1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:02,370
Hi everyone.

2
00:00:02,370 --> 00:00:08,676
So today we are going to continue where Andrej Karpathy's Zero to Hero series left off.

3
00:00:08,676 --> 00:00:14,837
If you've been following along, you've built micrograd, you've built makemore, you've

4
00:00:14,837 --> 00:00:21,288
built a GPT from scratch on tiny Shakespeare, and in the last video you reproduced GPT-2,

5
00:00:21,288 --> 00:00:23,752
the 124 million parameter version.

6
00:00:23,752 --> 00:00:27,304
And at the end of all that, you had a base model.

7
00:00:27,304 --> 00:00:29,551
A thing that can continue text.

8
00:00:29,551 --> 00:00:34,842
But if you actually try to talk to it, you'll find that it's not ChatGPT.

9
00:00:34,842 --> 00:00:36,436
It's not an assistant.

10
00:00:36,436 --> 00:00:38,176
It just sort of babbles.

11
00:00:38,176 --> 00:00:42,486
So the question for today is: what happens after pretraining?

12
00:00:42,486 --> 00:00:48,845
How do you go from a base model, an internet document simulator, to something that you can

13
00:00:48,845 --> 00:00:54,992
actually talk to, that answers your questions, that uses tools, and that can even count

14
00:00:54,992 --> 00:00:57,252
the number of r's in strawberry?

15
00:00:57,252 --> 00:01:03,117
This stage is called post-training, and today we are going to build all of it, from

16
00:01:03,117 --> 00:01:06,649
scratch, following Karpathy's nanochat repository.

17
00:01:06,649 --> 00:01:11,291
Now here is my goal for this video, and I want to be very explicit about it.

18
00:01:11,291 --> 00:01:16,727
When you're done, you should be able to reproduce every piece of nanochat's post-training

19
00:01:16,727 --> 00:01:18,315
yourself, without looking.

20
00:01:18,315 --> 00:01:23,445
Not just know the words, but have typed every line, and understand why every line is

21
00:01:23,445 --> 00:01:23,811
there.

22
00:01:23,811 --> 00:01:27,964
So we're going to do this the same way as in the rest of the series.

23
00:01:27,964 --> 00:01:33,156
We'll start from a problem, try the most obvious thing, see it fail, and then fix it.

24
00:01:33,156 --> 00:01:38,469
Every piece of code gets written in the notebook first and tested on tiny examples, and

25
00:01:38,469 --> 00:01:43,905
only at the end of each chapter will we look at the file in the repository, which will be

26
00:01:43,905 --> 00:01:45,859
the exact same code, cleaned up.

27
00:01:45,859 --> 00:01:47,189
Here's what you need.

28
00:01:47,189 --> 00:01:52,570
You need the base model from the GPT-2 video, and I've put that model, with two small

29
00:01:52,570 --> 00:01:56,558
additions that we'll write together today, in this gpt.py file.

30
00:01:56,558 --> 00:02:02,193
It's the same code, the token embeddings, the position embeddings, the twelve transformer

31
00:02:02,193 --> 00:02:06,118
blocks, the language model head, with the same weight sharing.

32
00:02:06,118 --> 00:02:11,563
And we'll load the original OpenAI weights into it, exactly like we did in that video.

33
00:02:11,563 --> 00:02:17,197
Pretraining is the expensive part of the pipeline, it needs a node of eight H100 GPUs for

34
00:02:17,197 --> 00:02:18,337
a couple of hours.

35
00:02:18,337 --> 00:02:23,972
But post-training is cheap, and everything today runs on a laptop CPU, or on a free Colab

36
00:02:23,972 --> 00:02:25,618
GPU if you want it faster.

37
00:02:25,618 --> 00:02:27,100
So here is the roadmap.

38
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:30,517
First, we'll look at what a base model actually does.

39
00:02:30,517 --> 00:02:34,450
Then we'll figure out how to turn a conversation into tokens.

40
00:02:34,450 --> 00:02:40,252
Then we'll build a mixture of training data, a dataloader for it, and we'll run supervised

41
00:02:40,252 --> 00:02:40,961
finetuning.

42
00:02:40,961 --> 00:02:46,505
Then we'll write an inference engine with a KV cache and a python tool, evaluations to

43
00:02:46,505 --> 00:02:52,308
measure what we did, reinforcement learning, where the model practices and learns from its

44
00:02:52,308 --> 00:02:58,045
own successes and failures, and finally preference optimization, for all the things where

45
00:02:58,045 --> 00:03:02,816
we can't check the answer, but we can tell which of two answers is better.

46
00:03:02,816 --> 00:03:05,585
And two things about how to use this video.

47
00:03:05,585 --> 00:03:11,060
Every now and then, a yellow box will pop up with a question, and a little countdown.

48
00:03:11,060 --> 00:03:16,599
That's your cue to pause, and actually commit to a guess before I show you the answer.

49
00:03:16,599 --> 00:03:20,721
You learn a lot more from a wrong guess than from nodding along.

50
00:03:20,721 --> 00:03:26,389
And for every piece we build, there's an exercise in the exercises notebook in the repo.

51
00:03:26,389 --> 00:03:31,992
You get the signature of a function, you write it, and a check runs it against the real

52
00:03:31,992 --> 00:03:34,633
thing and tells you exactly what's wrong.

53
00:03:34,633 --> 00:03:40,301
My recommendation is to watch a chapter, then close the video, and do its exercises from

54
00:03:40,301 --> 00:03:40,752
memory.

55
00:03:40,752 --> 00:03:45,067
When they all pass, you've built nanochat's post-training yourself.

56
00:03:45,067 --> 00:03:46,806
Okay, so let's get started.

57
00:03:48,306 --> 00:03:51,883
Okay so first, let's load up GPT-2 and sample from it.

58
00:03:51,883 --> 00:03:57,514
The Engine here is something we'll write ourselves later in the lecture, for now just

59
00:03:57,514 --> 00:04:01,555
think of it as a function that samples tokens from the model.

60
00:04:01,555 --> 00:04:07,186
And I'm writing a little complete function that takes a prompt, puts the beginning of

61
00:04:07,186 --> 00:04:13,149
sequence token in front of it, and samples greedily, so always the most likely next token.

62
00:04:14,149 --> 00:04:20,654
"Hello, I'm a language model, and I'm a programmer." You'll recognize this from the GPT-2

63
00:04:20,654 --> 00:04:26,574
video, it's exactly the same output, so we know the weights are loaded correctly.

64
00:04:26,574 --> 00:04:28,620
Now let's ask it a question.

65
00:04:28,620 --> 00:04:29,910
Pause and predict.

66
00:04:29,910 --> 00:04:33,419
This model was never trained to answer questions.

67
00:04:33,419 --> 00:04:37,502
What will it write after "What is the capital of France?"

68
00:04:42,502 --> 00:04:45,174
Okay, so actually this is not too bad.

69
00:04:45,174 --> 00:04:48,619
It does know that the capital of France is Paris.

70
00:04:48,619 --> 00:04:52,627
But then it says it again, and again, and again, forever.

71
00:04:52,627 --> 00:04:55,509
And I want you to really think about why.

72
00:04:55,509 --> 00:04:58,040
This model was trained on web pages.

73
00:04:58,040 --> 00:05:04,227
So when it sees a question, it's asking itself, statistically, what text comes next in a

74
00:05:04,227 --> 00:05:06,407
web page that starts like this.

75
00:05:06,407 --> 00:05:08,024
It's not answering you.

76
00:05:08,024 --> 00:05:09,922
It's simulating a document.

77
00:05:09,922 --> 00:05:16,110
And documents on the internet don't end after one sentence, so it has no notion of being

78
00:05:16,110 --> 00:05:16,461
done.

79
00:05:16,461 --> 00:05:21,284
Now because it's a document simulator, there's actually a fun trick.

80
00:05:21,284 --> 00:05:27,028
We can give it the beginning of a document that looks like a conversation with an

81
00:05:27,028 --> 00:05:30,220
assistant, and let it continue that document.

82
00:05:30,220 --> 00:05:32,631
This is called few-shot prompting.

83
00:05:33,631 --> 00:05:34,602
So look at this.

84
00:05:34,602 --> 00:05:38,909
It did pick up on the format, it answers in the voice of the Assistant.

85
00:05:38,909 --> 00:05:42,488
The answer is "France", which is wrong, it's a small model.

86
00:05:42,488 --> 00:05:45,460
But the much bigger problem is what happens next.

87
00:05:45,460 --> 00:05:50,799
It writes the next question from the Human, and then the answer of the Assistant, and so

88
00:05:50,799 --> 00:05:50,981
on.

89
00:05:50,981 --> 00:05:56,380
It's simulating the whole document, both sides of the conversation, because that's all it

90
00:05:56,380 --> 00:05:57,350
knows how to do.

91
00:05:57,350 --> 00:05:59,582
So this tells us three things.

92
00:05:59,582 --> 00:06:04,344
Number one, the knowledge is already in there, from pretraining.

93
00:06:04,344 --> 00:06:10,965
Number two, the model is perfectly happy to play an assistant, if the document looks like

94
00:06:10,965 --> 00:06:11,486
a chat.

95
00:06:11,486 --> 00:06:15,429
And number three, it has no idea where its turn ends.

96
00:06:15,429 --> 00:06:19,149
And that suggests the whole idea of post-training.

97
00:06:19,149 --> 00:06:25,250
Instead of tricking the model with a prompt, we just keep training it, on a lot of

98
00:06:25,250 --> 00:06:31,871
documents that are conversations, in some very specific format with clearly marked turns.

99
00:06:31,871 --> 00:06:36,038
Then conversations become the kind of document it knows.

100
00:06:36,038 --> 00:06:41,447
Exercise: Before we move on, think about how you would do this.

101
00:06:41,447 --> 00:06:49,174
If a conversation has to become a single sequence of tokens, what would that sequence look

102
00:06:49,174 --> 00:06:49,603
like?

103
00:06:49,603 --> 00:06:55,270
And which of those tokens should the model actually be trained on?

104
00:06:55,270 --> 00:06:58,189
Pause the video and give it a try.

105
00:07:01,689 --> 00:07:04,455
Okay so what is a conversation, as data?

106
00:07:04,455 --> 00:07:10,054
In nanochat, and in most datasets on HuggingFace, it's just a dict with a list of

107
00:07:10,054 --> 00:07:15,239
messages, and every message has a role, user or assistant, and the content.

108
00:07:15,239 --> 00:07:21,392
Our job is to turn that into one sequence of integers, so that we can train on it exactly

109
00:07:21,392 --> 00:07:23,397
like we trained on web pages.

110
00:07:23,397 --> 00:07:25,337
So, the obvious first idea.

111
00:07:25,337 --> 00:07:27,492
Why not just write it as text?

112
00:07:27,492 --> 00:07:29,648
"User: blah, Assistant: blah".

113
00:07:29,648 --> 00:07:32,234
That's what our few-shot prompt did.

114
00:07:32,234 --> 00:07:34,749
And there are two problems with it.

115
00:07:34,749 --> 00:07:40,856
First, the model can't tell a real turn boundary from the text "Assistant:" appearing

116
00:07:40,856 --> 00:07:42,077
inside a message.

117
00:07:42,077 --> 00:07:47,897
And second, anyone could type "Assistant:" in their message and pretend to be the

118
00:07:47,897 --> 00:07:48,615
assistant.

119
00:07:48,615 --> 00:07:55,009
So instead, we're going to mark the turns with brand new special tokens, tokens that only

120
00:07:55,009 --> 00:07:56,015
we can insert.

121
00:07:56,015 --> 00:08:02,481
A user start and a user end around everything the user says, and an assistant start and an

122
00:08:02,481 --> 00:08:06,146
assistant end around everything the assistant says.

123
00:08:06,146 --> 00:08:08,876
Let's see why they need to be special.

124
00:08:08,876 --> 00:08:13,474
Here's what the GPT-2 tokenizer does with the text of our token.

125
00:08:13,474 --> 00:08:15,161
Pause and predict.

126
00:08:15,161 --> 00:08:21,911
Typed out as plain text, how many GPT-2 tokens is our user start marker?

127
00:08:26,911 --> 00:08:28,547
Seven ordinary tokens.

128
00:08:28,547 --> 00:08:32,340
A less than sign, a pipe, the word user, and so on.

129
00:08:32,340 --> 00:08:33,455
That's no good.

130
00:08:33,455 --> 00:08:39,999
We want each of our markers to be exactly one token, one integer that can never come out

131
00:08:39,999 --> 00:08:41,115
of normal text.

132
00:08:41,115 --> 00:08:46,320
And tiktoken lets us do that, we just register them as special tokens.

133
00:08:47,320 --> 00:08:53,935
So GPT-2 already has one special token, end of text, with id 50,256, and we'll use it as

134
00:08:53,935 --> 00:08:56,717
our beginning of sequence token, BOS.

135
00:08:56,717 --> 00:09:02,355
And we give our eight new tokens the next eight ids, 50,257 through 50,264.

136
00:09:02,355 --> 00:09:08,894
You'll also notice there are two more pairs, python start and end, and output start and

137
00:09:08,894 --> 00:09:09,195
end.

138
00:09:09,195 --> 00:09:13,329
Those are for tool use, and we'll get to them in a bit.

139
00:09:13,329 --> 00:09:17,539
Now if we ask for the special token, we get a single id.

140
00:09:17,539 --> 00:09:24,229
But encode_ordinary, which is what we'll use for everything a user types, still treats it

141
00:09:24,229 --> 00:09:25,282
as plain text.

142
00:09:25,282 --> 00:09:28,213
So a user can't inject a turn boundary.

143
00:09:28,213 --> 00:09:30,318
That's exactly what we want.

144
00:09:30,318 --> 00:09:35,942
Okay, now let's write the function that renders a conversation into tokens.

145
00:09:35,942 --> 00:09:42,465
Simplest version first: BOS, and then for every message, the start token, the tokens of

146
00:09:42,465 --> 00:09:44,790
the content, and the end token.

147
00:09:45,790 --> 00:09:50,358
So the whole conversation is thirteen tokens.

148
00:09:50,358 --> 00:09:58,987
And the decoded pieces look right: BOS, user start, the question, user end, assistant

149
00:09:58,987 --> 00:10:01,829
start, Paris, assistant end.

150
00:10:01,829 --> 00:10:05,814
Now here's the important question, the one from the exercise.

151
00:10:05,814 --> 00:10:11,367
If we train on this sequence like we train on a web page, the model learns to predict

152
00:10:11,367 --> 00:10:15,090
every one of these tokens, including the user's question.

153
00:10:15,090 --> 00:10:16,462
Is that what we want?

154
00:10:16,462 --> 00:10:17,442
Think about it.

155
00:10:17,442 --> 00:10:22,799
We don't want a model that simulates users, we want a model that responds to them.

156
00:10:22,799 --> 00:10:28,547
Predicting what people ask is not useless, but it's not our job, and with a small model,

157
00:10:28,547 --> 00:10:30,507
every bit of capacity matters.

158
00:10:30,507 --> 00:10:34,753
So we only want to train on the tokens that the assistant writes.

159
00:10:34,753 --> 00:10:40,567
And the way we do that is we return a second list, a mask, with a one for every token the

160
00:10:40,567 --> 00:10:44,879
assistant should learn to produce, and a zero for everything else.

161
00:10:45,879 --> 00:10:51,036
So everything the user wrote, and all the special tokens around it, get a zero.

162
00:10:51,036 --> 00:10:56,846
The assistant start token also gets a zero, because we always put it there ourselves, the

163
00:10:56,846 --> 00:10:58,804
model never has to produce it.

164
00:10:58,804 --> 00:11:03,243
Then the content gets a one, and then assistant end also gets a one.

165
00:11:03,243 --> 00:11:05,398
And this one is really important.

166
00:11:05,398 --> 00:11:08,335
This is how the model learns to stop talking.

167
00:11:08,335 --> 00:11:11,273
Remember the base model that went on forever?

168
00:11:11,273 --> 00:11:12,317
This is the fix.

169
00:11:12,317 --> 00:11:17,997
After Paris, the model must learn to emit assistant end, and then we stop sampling, and

170
00:11:17,997 --> 00:11:19,368
it's the user's turn.

171
00:11:19,368 --> 00:11:20,698
Now for tool use.

172
00:11:20,698 --> 00:11:27,034
Sometimes we want the assistant to run a bit of python, for example a calculator.

173
00:11:27,034 --> 00:11:32,276
So the content of an assistant message can also be a list of parts.

174
00:11:32,276 --> 00:11:34,232
A text part is just text.

175
00:11:34,232 --> 00:11:41,272
A python part is code that the assistant writes, and we wrap it in python start and python

176
00:11:41,272 --> 00:11:41,585
end.

177
00:11:41,585 --> 00:11:48,470
And a python output part is the result of running that code, wrapped in output start and

178
00:11:48,470 --> 00:11:49,330
output end.

179
00:11:49,330 --> 00:11:50,579
Pause and predict.

180
00:11:50,579 --> 00:11:54,951
The model writes the python code, and python writes the output.

181
00:11:54,951 --> 00:11:58,212
Which of these tokens should get a mask of one?

182
00:12:03,212 --> 00:12:07,606
The code gets a one, because at test time the model has to write it.

183
00:12:07,606 --> 00:12:09,351
But the output gets a zero.

184
00:12:09,351 --> 00:12:13,810
At test time, python is going to produce those tokens, not the model.

185
00:12:13,810 --> 00:12:19,627
If we trained on them, we would be teaching the model to predict what python would say, in

186
00:12:19,627 --> 00:12:24,086
other words to hallucinate tool outputs, and that's actively harmful.

187
00:12:24,086 --> 00:12:29,773
And then after the output, the model continues in green, and it can copy the result into

188
00:12:29,773 --> 00:12:30,484
its answer.

189
00:12:30,484 --> 00:12:32,039
And one last function.

190
00:12:32,039 --> 00:12:36,919
At test time, we have a conversation where it's the assistant's turn.

191
00:12:36,919 --> 00:12:43,071
So we render everything except the last assistant message, and then we append assistant

192
00:12:43,071 --> 00:12:43,496
start.

193
00:12:43,496 --> 00:12:45,051
And that's our prompt.

194
00:12:45,051 --> 00:12:50,284
The model takes it from there, and we sample until it emits assistant end.

195
00:12:51,284 --> 00:12:56,561
So the prompt ends with assistant start, and the next token the model writes is the

196
00:12:56,561 --> 00:12:58,087
beginning of its answer.

197
00:12:58,087 --> 00:13:03,745
Now, I've put exactly this code into the tokenizer file of the repo, and let's just check

198
00:13:03,745 --> 00:13:07,115
that our notebook version and the repo version agree.

199
00:13:08,115 --> 00:13:08,449
Good.

200
00:13:08,449 --> 00:13:14,267
The repo version, in tokenizer.py, is the same function, with two small additions: if a

201
00:13:14,267 --> 00:13:20,085
conversation starts with a system message, we just glue it onto the first user message,

202
00:13:20,085 --> 00:13:23,896
because we don't have special tokens for system messages.

203
00:13:23,896 --> 00:13:29,915
And there's a max tokens argument that truncates, which, I'll tell you right now, is going

204
00:13:29,915 --> 00:13:31,052
to bite us later.

205
00:13:31,052 --> 00:13:34,596
And by the way, this exact format is a design choice.

206
00:13:34,596 --> 00:13:40,481
OpenAI's ChatML uses tokens like im start and im end, Llama has its own, and they all do

207
00:13:40,481 --> 00:13:42,152
basically the same thing.

208
00:13:42,152 --> 00:13:47,970
What matters is that you render conversations the same way at training time and at test

209
00:13:47,970 --> 00:13:48,305
time.

210
00:13:48,305 --> 00:13:52,183
Even a single space different can quietly hurt your model.

211
00:13:52,183 --> 00:13:58,889
Exercise: Add support for system messages to your render_conversation, the way the repo

212
00:13:58,889 --> 00:13:59,506
does it.

213
00:13:59,506 --> 00:14:06,211
Then write a conversation with two user turns and two assistant turns and visualize it.

214
00:14:06,211 --> 00:14:07,984
Which tokens are green?

215
00:14:07,984 --> 00:14:14,844
Then, in the exercises notebook, write render conversation and render for completion from

216
00:14:14,844 --> 00:14:19,238
scratch, exercises one and two, and make the checks pass.

217
00:14:19,238 --> 00:14:21,858
Pause the video and give it a try.

218
00:14:25,358 --> 00:14:31,260
Okay so we have our token ids, but now we have a problem.

219
00:14:31,260 --> 00:14:40,474
GPT-2's vocabulary has 50,257 tokens, so its embedding table has exactly 50,257 rows, and

220
00:14:40,474 --> 00:14:45,650
our special tokens have ids 50,257 through 50,264.

221
00:14:45,650 --> 00:14:48,135
There's no row for them.

222
00:14:48,135 --> 00:14:56,313
If we feed id 50,257 into the original GPT-2, it's an index out of range error.

223
00:14:56,313 --> 00:15:02,584
Now if you remember the GPT-2 reproduction video, we padded the vocab size from 50,257 up

224
00:15:02,584 --> 00:15:08,715
to 50,304, because it's a nice number, divisible by 128, and the GPU kernels like that.

225
00:15:08,715 --> 00:15:12,026
And we said those extra 47 rows are never used.

226
00:15:12,026 --> 00:15:13,858
Well, today they get used.

227
00:15:13,858 --> 00:15:20,129
So when we load the OpenAI weights, we copy their 50,257 rows, and we have to decide what

228
00:15:20,129 --> 00:15:21,750
to put in the new rows.

229
00:15:21,750 --> 00:15:22,877
Here's the code.

230
00:15:22,877 --> 00:15:29,148
Copy GPT-2's rows, and then, for all the new rows, I'm using the average of all the token

231
00:15:29,148 --> 00:15:29,923
embeddings.

232
00:15:29,923 --> 00:15:31,051
Why the average?

233
00:15:31,051 --> 00:15:37,040
Because random vectors could be anywhere, but the average sort of lives in the region

234
00:15:37,040 --> 00:15:41,056
where real embeddings live, so it won't blow anything up.

235
00:15:41,056 --> 00:15:43,804
Let's check that it did what we wanted.

236
00:15:44,804 --> 00:15:46,765
So the table has 50,304 rows.

237
00:15:46,765 --> 00:15:52,646
The language model head is literally the same tensor as the embedding table, that's the

238
00:15:52,646 --> 00:15:55,080
weight sharing from the GPT-2 paper.

239
00:15:55,080 --> 00:16:00,556
And that matters a lot here, because it means the new rows are not only the input

240
00:16:00,556 --> 00:16:06,235
embeddings of our special tokens, they're also the output weights that produce their

241
00:16:06,235 --> 00:16:06,709
logits.

242
00:16:06,709 --> 00:16:09,751
And all the new rows are exactly the average.

243
00:16:09,751 --> 00:16:16,748
Now, and this is something that Karpathy drills into you in the makemore videos, before we

244
00:16:16,748 --> 00:16:21,568
train anything, let's sanity check the loss at initialization.

245
00:16:21,568 --> 00:16:22,967
What do we expect?

246
00:16:22,967 --> 00:16:29,808
If the model had no idea and spread its probability evenly over the vocabulary, the loss

247
00:16:29,808 --> 00:16:33,929
would be minus log of one over 50,304, so about 10.8.

248
00:16:33,929 --> 00:16:38,982
Let's look at the loss of every single token of our conversation.

249
00:16:38,982 --> 00:16:40,546
Pause and predict.

250
00:16:40,546 --> 00:16:44,282
The new rows are all the average embedding.

251
00:16:44,282 --> 00:16:51,233
Will the loss on the special tokens be above or below the uniform guess of 10.8?

252
00:16:56,233 --> 00:16:58,052
So 10.8 is the uniform guess.

253
00:16:58,052 --> 00:17:03,696
The normal English tokens look fine, the model is quite sure that "is" follows "What", and

254
00:17:03,696 --> 00:17:04,072
so on.

255
00:17:04,072 --> 00:17:06,016
But look at the special tokens.

256
00:17:06,016 --> 00:17:10,280
All of them are around 15, which is much worse than a uniform guess.

257
00:17:10,280 --> 00:17:12,914
The model is confidently wrong about them.

258
00:17:12,914 --> 00:17:15,359
And it's because of the weight sharing.

259
00:17:15,359 --> 00:17:20,689
The logit of a special token is the dot product of the hidden state with that average

260
00:17:20,689 --> 00:17:25,957
embedding, and that just happens to be a small number compared to the logits of real

261
00:17:25,957 --> 00:17:26,396
tokens.

262
00:17:26,396 --> 00:17:29,469
So the probability is tiny, and the loss is huge.

263
00:17:29,469 --> 00:17:35,112
And what this means is that at the start of finetuning, a lot of the loss is going to come

264
00:17:35,112 --> 00:17:40,254
from these tokens, and the first thing the model will do is learn that they exist.

265
00:17:40,254 --> 00:17:43,515
It's the hockey stick from makemore, all over again.

266
00:17:43,515 --> 00:17:50,066
Exercise: Can you find a better initialization for the new rows, one that gives a loss of

267
00:17:50,066 --> 00:17:54,555
about 10.8 or lower for the special tokens at initialization?

268
00:17:54,555 --> 00:18:00,738
Try initializing assistant end to the embedding of a newline, or scaling the average

269
00:18:00,738 --> 00:18:01,253
vector.

270
00:18:01,253 --> 00:18:03,976
Measure the loss with the cell above.

271
00:18:03,976 --> 00:18:10,453
And exercise three in the notebook is the surgery itself: expanding the embedding table.

272
00:18:10,453 --> 00:18:12,955
Pause the video and give it a try.

273
00:18:16,455 --> 00:18:19,641
Okay, now we need conversations to train on.

274
00:18:19,641 --> 00:18:20,654
A lot of them.

275
00:18:20,654 --> 00:18:26,881
And here's the key idea of the SFT stage in nanochat: the data is a mixture of several

276
00:18:26,881 --> 00:18:31,876
datasets, and every one of them teaches the model one specific thing.

277
00:18:31,876 --> 00:18:34,338
So let's build them one at a time.

278
00:18:34,338 --> 00:18:40,853
And the one I find the most instructive is grade school math, GSM8K, so let's start there.

279
00:18:40,853 --> 00:18:46,184
All of these datasets live on HuggingFace, and every dataset on HuggingFace has an

280
00:18:46,184 --> 00:18:48,395
automatic export to parquet files.

281
00:18:48,395 --> 00:18:54,246
So I wrote a tiny function that downloads those parquet files and reads them with pyarrow.

282
00:18:54,246 --> 00:18:57,171
You don't need the datasets library for this.

283
00:18:57,171 --> 00:19:00,942
Let's load the GSM8K training set and look at one problem.

284
00:19:01,942 --> 00:19:06,067
7,473 problems, each with a question and an answer.

285
00:19:06,067 --> 00:19:07,927
And look at the answer.

286
00:19:07,927 --> 00:19:11,729
What are these things in double angle brackets?

287
00:19:11,729 --> 00:19:17,472
These are calculator annotations, from the people who made the dataset.

288
00:19:17,472 --> 00:19:23,215
"12 times 2 equals 24" is written out as the expression and its result.

289
00:19:23,215 --> 00:19:30,333
And at the very end, after four hash signs, is the final answer, which is what we'll use

290
00:19:30,333 --> 00:19:31,303
for grading.

291
00:19:31,303 --> 00:19:32,662
So here's the plan.

292
00:19:32,662 --> 00:19:35,522
These calculator annotations are a gift.

293
00:19:35,522 --> 00:19:40,027
They're exactly the tool calls that we want our model to learn.

294
00:19:40,027 --> 00:19:46,319
So we're going to turn every one of them into a python part, followed by a python output

295
00:19:46,319 --> 00:19:46,677
part.

296
00:19:46,677 --> 00:19:51,468
Step one, let's split the answer into the text and the annotations.

297
00:19:51,468 --> 00:19:56,616
A regex split with a capture group keeps the separators, so we get both.

298
00:19:57,616 --> 00:20:02,845
So we get the plain text, then the annotation, then text again, alternating.

299
00:20:02,845 --> 00:20:08,899
Step two, for every annotation, we strip the angle brackets and split at the last equals

300
00:20:08,899 --> 00:20:09,243
sign.

301
00:20:09,243 --> 00:20:15,366
Left of it is the expression, which becomes a python part, and right of it is the result,

302
00:20:15,366 --> 00:20:17,774
which becomes a python output part.

303
00:20:17,774 --> 00:20:19,906
Everything else is a text part.

304
00:20:19,906 --> 00:20:25,410
And then we can render it with our own render_conversation and look at the mask.

305
00:20:26,410 --> 00:20:32,063
So the model is trained to write the expression, in green, the result is red, it will come

306
00:20:32,063 --> 00:20:36,335
from python, and then the model copies the result into its sentence.

307
00:20:36,335 --> 00:20:39,476
This is the simplest possible version of tool use.

308
00:20:39,476 --> 00:20:44,815
The model learns to hand the arithmetic, which it's bad at, to a calculator, which is

309
00:20:44,815 --> 00:20:45,694
perfect at it.

310
00:20:45,694 --> 00:20:48,898
And at the end, the four hash signs and the answer.

311
00:20:48,898 --> 00:20:54,363
Now to grade an answer, we just pull out the number after the hash signs, with a regex,

312
00:20:54,363 --> 00:20:56,373
and compare it to the reference.

313
00:20:57,373 --> 00:21:04,386
So 42 for our problem, commas get removed so 1,024 becomes 1024, and None when there's no

314
00:21:04,386 --> 00:21:05,490
answer at all.

315
00:21:05,490 --> 00:21:07,775
That's it, that's our grader.

316
00:21:07,775 --> 00:21:14,631
And notice that this grader is going to be our reward in reinforcement learning, at the

317
00:21:14,631 --> 00:21:16,129
end of the lecture.

318
00:21:16,129 --> 00:21:20,096
The second dataset is MMLU, which is multiple choice questions.

319
00:21:20,096 --> 00:21:25,511
And it teaches the model the format of multiple choice, which is how we'll evaluate it

320
00:21:25,511 --> 00:21:25,889
later.

321
00:21:25,889 --> 00:21:31,368
Let's write the function that renders a multiple choice question, and I'll show you two

322
00:21:31,368 --> 00:21:32,690
little details in it.

323
00:21:33,690 --> 00:21:38,577
The first detail is that the letter comes after the choice, not before it.

324
00:21:38,577 --> 00:21:44,455
Big models don't care, but for small models it helps to have the letter right next to the

325
00:21:44,455 --> 00:21:45,511
thing it labels.

326
00:21:45,511 --> 00:21:50,926
The second detail is that there's no space between the equals sign and the letter.

327
00:21:50,926 --> 00:21:51,190
Why?

328
00:21:51,190 --> 00:21:52,445
Look at the tokens.

329
00:21:52,445 --> 00:21:56,408
"Space A" is token 317, and "A" without a space is token 32.

330
00:21:56,408 --> 00:22:02,087
These are completely different tokens as far as the model is concerned, just different

331
00:22:02,087 --> 00:22:02,681
integers.

332
00:22:02,681 --> 00:22:05,719
The assistant answers with just "A", token 32.

333
00:22:05,719 --> 00:22:11,530
So in the prompt, we want the exact same token 32 to appear right next to the choice, so

334
00:22:11,530 --> 00:22:13,776
the model can sort of point at it.

335
00:22:13,776 --> 00:22:19,059
If you remember the tokenization video, so much weirdness in LLMs comes from the

336
00:22:19,059 --> 00:22:21,964
tokenizer, and this is one of those details.

337
00:22:21,964 --> 00:22:25,644
The third dataset is SmolTalk, from HuggingFace.

338
00:22:25,644 --> 00:22:32,005
That's 460,000 general conversations, and it's where most of the personality of the

339
00:22:32,005 --> 00:22:33,615
assistant comes from.

340
00:22:34,615 --> 00:22:39,573
These were mostly generated by bigger language models, and then filtered.

341
00:22:39,573 --> 00:22:45,549
And it's worth pausing on this, because Karpathy makes a really important point about it

342
00:22:45,549 --> 00:22:46,431
in his talks.

343
00:22:46,431 --> 00:22:50,370
When you talk to ChatGPT, who are you actually talking to?

344
00:22:50,370 --> 00:22:54,716
You're talking to a statistical simulation of this kind of data.

345
00:22:54,716 --> 00:23:00,557
Originally it was written by human labelers following long instructions from a company

346
00:23:00,557 --> 00:23:06,261
about being helpful and harmless, and nowadays a lot of it is written by other LLMs.

347
00:23:06,261 --> 00:23:12,237
But either way, the knowledge comes from pretraining, and the personality, the tone, the

348
00:23:12,237 --> 00:23:16,176
length of the answers, all comes from imitating this data.

349
00:23:16,176 --> 00:23:19,215
And the fourth dataset is my favorite.

350
00:23:19,215 --> 00:23:22,174
How many r's are there in strawberry?

351
00:23:22,174 --> 00:23:27,052
Famously, LLMs get this wrong, and now we can understand why.

352
00:23:27,052 --> 00:23:29,211
Look at how it's tokenized.

353
00:23:29,211 --> 00:23:30,566
Pause and predict.

354
00:23:30,566 --> 00:23:34,780
How many tokens is " strawberry", with a space in front?

355
00:23:34,780 --> 00:23:37,413
And "strawberry" without the space?

356
00:23:42,413 --> 00:23:46,071
With a space in front, strawberry is a single token.

357
00:23:46,071 --> 00:23:46,915
One integer.

358
00:23:46,915 --> 00:23:49,236
The model never sees the letters!

359
00:23:49,236 --> 00:23:55,426
To count the r's, it would have to have memorized the spelling of every one of its fifty

360
00:23:55,426 --> 00:24:00,349
thousand tokens, and then count in its head, in a single forward pass.

361
00:24:00,349 --> 00:24:06,468
And without a space, it's three tokens, which is a completely different set of integers

362
00:24:06,468 --> 00:24:06,890
again.

363
00:24:06,890 --> 00:24:10,829
So nanochat has a synthetic task for this, called SpellingBee.

364
00:24:10,829 --> 00:24:15,086
Synthetic means we generate the data ourselves, as much as we want.

365
00:24:15,086 --> 00:24:18,581
And the interesting part is designing the ideal answer.

366
00:24:18,581 --> 00:24:24,172
We know the model can't do this in one step, so we write out a procedure that it can do:

367
00:24:24,172 --> 00:24:29,699
spell the word out, then go through the letters one at a time and keep a running count,

368
00:24:29,699 --> 00:24:33,575
then double check with python, and only then give the answer.

369
00:24:33,575 --> 00:24:34,528
Let's write it.

370
00:24:35,528 --> 00:24:36,801
First, the spelling.

371
00:24:36,801 --> 00:24:42,530
Then the counting, one letter per line, with a running count, so that no single token ever

372
00:24:42,530 --> 00:24:43,995
needs much computation.

373
00:24:43,995 --> 00:24:49,470
Then python checks it, and the answer goes after the hash signs, so the same grader as

374
00:24:49,470 --> 00:24:50,233
GSM8K works.

375
00:24:50,233 --> 00:24:55,899
And I think this is a really nice example of how you have to think about the cognition of

376
00:24:55,899 --> 00:24:56,536
the model.

377
00:24:56,536 --> 00:25:02,138
Every token only gets a fixed amount of compute, one forward pass through twelve layers.

378
00:25:02,138 --> 00:25:05,894
So you want to spread the computation out over many tokens.

379
00:25:05,894 --> 00:25:11,433
If you ask the model to blurt out the answer in the first token, you're asking it to do

380
00:25:11,433 --> 00:25:14,934
all of the work in one forward pass, and it just can't.

381
00:25:14,934 --> 00:25:20,218
In the repo, SpellingBee is this same function, plus a random word out of a list of

382
00:25:20,218 --> 00:25:25,502
370,000 English words, a random letter, and a bunch of different ways of asking the

383
00:25:25,502 --> 00:25:26,075
question.

384
00:25:26,075 --> 00:25:30,447
Okay, and the last piece is to mix all of these together.

385
00:25:30,447 --> 00:25:37,198
A mixture is just a big list of every example in every task, shuffled with a fixed seed.

386
00:25:37,198 --> 00:25:41,800
And if you want more of something, you just put it in twice.

387
00:25:42,800 --> 00:25:47,570
So about 75,000 conversations, and the tasks are nicely interleaved.

388
00:25:47,570 --> 00:25:53,462
Now, I'm using small slices of SmolTalk and MMLU here, because we'll train on a CPU.

389
00:25:53,462 --> 00:25:59,705
In nanochat, on a GPU, it's all 460,000 SmolTalk conversations, and here's the mixture in

390
00:25:59,705 --> 00:26:01,108
our chat_sft script.

391
00:26:01,108 --> 00:26:06,579
And remember what this mixture looks like, because it's going to matter later.

392
00:26:06,579 --> 00:26:11,770
Twenty thousand SpellingBee examples is more than a quarter of everything.

393
00:26:11,770 --> 00:26:15,039
Exercise: Write your own synthetic task.

394
00:26:15,039 --> 00:26:21,905
For example, a task that teaches the model its own name and who made it, or one that

395
00:26:21,905 --> 00:26:24,602
reverses a word letter by letter.

396
00:26:24,602 --> 00:26:31,958
Generate the ideal answer with code, and think about how to spread the work across tokens.

397
00:26:31,958 --> 00:26:39,313
And exercises four to seven in the notebook are the four pieces of this chapter: the GSM8K

398
00:26:39,313 --> 00:26:44,544
parser, the grader, multiple choice, and the SpellingBee answer.

399
00:26:44,544 --> 00:26:47,323
Pause the video and give it a try.

400
00:26:50,823 --> 00:26:54,337
Okay, so now we need to turn conversations into batches.

401
00:26:54,337 --> 00:26:57,287
And in the pretraining videos this was trivial.

402
00:26:57,287 --> 00:27:02,496
We had one endless stream of text, and we just chopped it into rows of 1024 tokens.

403
00:27:02,496 --> 00:27:04,630
A document cut in half, who cares.

404
00:27:04,630 --> 00:27:06,638
But a conversation is different.

405
00:27:06,638 --> 00:27:12,098
If you cut it in half, you get a question without its answer, and an answer without its

406
00:27:12,098 --> 00:27:12,663
question.

407
00:27:12,663 --> 00:27:17,997
So we want every conversation in one piece, and we want every row to start with a BOS

408
00:27:17,997 --> 00:27:21,700
token, because that's what the model will see at test time.

409
00:27:21,700 --> 00:27:26,225
So the first question is, how long are conversations?

410
00:27:26,225 --> 00:27:27,420
Let's measure.

411
00:27:28,420 --> 00:27:31,835
Half of them are shorter than about 200 tokens.

412
00:27:31,835 --> 00:27:37,864
But there's a long tail, ten percent are over a thousand tokens, and the longest is

413
00:27:37,864 --> 00:27:38,372
20,000.

414
00:27:38,372 --> 00:27:44,765
And with the rows of 512 tokens we're going to use on the CPU, one in five conversations

415
00:27:44,765 --> 00:27:45,491
won't fit.

416
00:27:45,491 --> 00:27:47,307
Keep that number in mind.

417
00:27:47,307 --> 00:27:52,996
Okay so the simplest thing we could do is one conversation per row, and fill up the rest

418
00:27:52,996 --> 00:27:54,548
of the row with padding.

419
00:27:54,548 --> 00:27:58,491
And we don't train on the padding, so it gets a mask of zero.

420
00:27:58,491 --> 00:28:01,465
Let's measure how much of the batch is wasted.

421
00:28:01,465 --> 00:28:03,035
Pause and predict.

422
00:28:03,035 --> 00:28:09,924
Half the conversations are shorter than about 200 tokens, and the rows are 512.

423
00:28:09,924 --> 00:28:13,586
What fraction of a naive batch is padding?

424
00:28:18,586 --> 00:28:21,535
47 percent of every batch is padding.

425
00:28:21,535 --> 00:28:25,202
Half of our compute would be spent on nothing.

426
00:28:25,202 --> 00:28:29,745
And only a quarter of the tokens are actually trained on.

427
00:28:29,745 --> 00:28:31,020
That's terrible.

428
00:28:31,020 --> 00:28:33,060
So the better idea is packing.

429
00:28:33,060 --> 00:28:36,460
We put several whole conversations into every row.

430
00:28:36,460 --> 00:28:39,928
And the algorithm nanochat uses is called best fit.

431
00:28:39,928 --> 00:28:45,640
We keep a buffer of a hundred conversations, and to fill a row, we keep grabbing the

432
00:28:45,640 --> 00:28:50,808
longest conversation in the buffer that still fits in the space that's left.

433
00:28:50,808 --> 00:28:55,092
And only when nothing fits anymore, we pad the rest of the row.

434
00:28:55,092 --> 00:28:59,512
Long conversations go first, and the short ones fill in the gaps.

435
00:28:59,512 --> 00:29:01,688
And we never cut a conversation.

436
00:29:01,688 --> 00:29:07,407
But before I show you the code, I want to tell you about a bug I hit writing this, because

437
00:29:07,407 --> 00:29:08,932
it's really instructive.

438
00:29:08,932 --> 00:29:14,587
In my first version, I rendered every conversation with max tokens set to the row length,

439
00:29:14,587 --> 00:29:17,193
so long conversations just got truncated.

440
00:29:17,193 --> 00:29:18,591
Seems harmless, right?

441
00:29:18,591 --> 00:29:22,403
Let's see what that does to a long multiple choice question.

442
00:29:22,403 --> 00:29:24,015
Pause and predict.

443
00:29:24,015 --> 00:29:29,297
A long multiple choice question gets cut off at 513 tokens.

444
00:29:29,297 --> 00:29:32,878
How many of its tokens will we train on?

445
00:29:37,878 --> 00:29:40,735
513 tokens, and a mask that sums to zero.

446
00:29:40,735 --> 00:29:42,755
Not a single trainable token!

447
00:29:42,755 --> 00:29:49,026
These MMLU training questions include long reading passages, and the answer, the one green

448
00:29:49,026 --> 00:29:50,838
token, is at the very end.

449
00:29:50,838 --> 00:29:56,063
So truncation cut off the answer, and the whole conversation became prompt.

450
00:29:56,063 --> 00:29:57,805
And here's the evil part.

451
00:29:57,805 --> 00:30:03,379
The best fit algorithm loves these, because they're exactly the length of a row.

452
00:30:03,379 --> 00:30:09,510
So it filled whole batches with them, and the loss on a batch with zero trainable tokens

453
00:30:09,510 --> 00:30:12,784
is zero divided by zero, which is not a number.

454
00:30:12,784 --> 00:30:16,895
My loss was NaN, and it took me a minute to figure out why.

455
00:30:16,895 --> 00:30:21,981
So the fix is simple: if a conversation doesn't fit in a row, we skip it.

456
00:30:21,981 --> 00:30:24,559
Okay, now let's write the real thing.

457
00:30:25,559 --> 00:30:26,743
So let's read it.

458
00:30:26,743 --> 00:30:32,382
The rows have one extra token, because the targets are the inputs shifted by one.

459
00:30:32,382 --> 00:30:37,047
Here's the fix for my bug, we skip conversations that are too long.

460
00:30:37,047 --> 00:30:40,041
Here's best fit, the longest one that fits.

461
00:30:40,041 --> 00:30:42,269
The padding gets a mask of zero.

462
00:30:42,269 --> 00:30:45,959
Then inputs and targets, exactly like in pretraining.

463
00:30:45,959 --> 00:30:50,275
And wherever the mask is zero, we set the target to minus one.

464
00:30:50,275 --> 00:30:55,358
And minus one is the ignore index of the cross entropy loss in our model.

465
00:30:55,358 --> 00:30:58,003
So those positions simply don't count.

466
00:30:58,003 --> 00:31:03,645
With packing, the padding goes down to about 8 percent, and that even includes the BOS

467
00:31:03,645 --> 00:31:06,400
tokens at the start of every conversation.

468
00:31:06,400 --> 00:31:10,270
And 42 percent of the tokens are trained on, instead of 25.

469
00:31:10,270 --> 00:31:14,010
That's almost twice as much signal from the same compute.

470
00:31:14,010 --> 00:31:19,717
Let's do one more sanity check, on our tiny France conversation, and look at the inputs

471
00:31:19,717 --> 00:31:21,357
and targets side by side.

472
00:31:22,357 --> 00:31:26,485
So the inputs are the conversation without its last token.

473
00:31:26,485 --> 00:31:31,823
And the targets are minus one everywhere, except at the last two positions.

474
00:31:31,823 --> 00:31:38,158
After assistant start, the target is Paris, and after Paris, the target is assistant end.

475
00:31:38,158 --> 00:31:42,784
Those are the only two predictions that this conversation trains.

476
00:31:42,784 --> 00:31:48,747
Exercise: Change the dataloader so that a conversation that's too long gets cropped in a

477
00:31:48,747 --> 00:31:53,693
smarter way, keeping the last assistant answer, instead of being skipped.

478
00:31:53,693 --> 00:31:54,710
Is it worth it?

479
00:31:54,710 --> 00:31:57,488
Measure how many trained tokens you gain.

480
00:31:57,488 --> 00:32:03,518
But first, do exercise eight in the notebook: write the best fit dataloader from scratch.

481
00:32:03,518 --> 00:32:09,006
The check will catch a cropped conversation, a wrong target, or too much padding.

482
00:32:09,006 --> 00:32:11,310
Pause the video and give it a try.

483
00:32:14,810 --> 00:32:17,973
Okay, and now the punchline of this whole stage.

484
00:32:17,973 --> 00:32:22,057
SFT is literally just pretraining with a different dataloader.

485
00:32:22,057 --> 00:32:27,196
Same model, same cross entropy loss, same AdamW optimizer, same training loop.

486
00:32:27,196 --> 00:32:32,730
The only differences are the minus ones in the targets, and a gentler learning rate.

487
00:32:32,730 --> 00:32:38,330
The model already knows a lot, we're just nudging its statistics, so we use a smaller

488
00:32:38,330 --> 00:32:44,193
learning rate than in pretraining, keep it constant for the first half, and then decay it

489
00:32:44,193 --> 00:32:44,720
to zero.

490
00:32:45,720 --> 00:32:49,869
Constant for the first half, then down to zero at the end.

491
00:32:49,869 --> 00:32:52,301
That's the schedule nanochat uses.

492
00:32:52,301 --> 00:32:56,450
And now, let's actually train, right here in the notebook.

493
00:32:56,450 --> 00:33:02,387
A fresh copy of GPT-2, AdamW, our dataloader with small rows of 256 tokens, and the

494
00:33:02,387 --> 00:33:05,678
training loop you've written ten times by now.

495
00:33:06,678 --> 00:33:08,755
And the loss is going down.

496
00:33:08,755 --> 00:33:11,140
From 3.4 to 1.6 in eight steps.

497
00:33:11,140 --> 00:33:17,603
And notice how the number of trained tokens jumps around from batch to batch, that's

498
00:33:17,603 --> 00:33:24,450
because every batch has a different mix of conversations, so the loss of a single step is

499
00:33:24,450 --> 00:33:24,911
noisy.

500
00:33:24,911 --> 00:33:25,945
So that's SFT.

501
00:33:25,945 --> 00:33:28,232
Everything else is engineering.

502
00:33:28,232 --> 00:33:34,579
I've put the dataloader, the schedule and the loop into chat_sft.py, plus command line

503
00:33:34,579 --> 00:33:40,852
arguments, a validation loss every hundred steps, gradient accumulation, bfloat16 and

504
00:33:40,852 --> 00:33:45,353
torch compile on a GPU, and saving the checkpoint at the end.

505
00:33:45,353 --> 00:33:51,552
In nanochat, you do one full epoch over the whole mixture, which on a single A100 is

506
00:33:51,552 --> 00:33:53,619
something like half an hour.

507
00:33:53,619 --> 00:34:00,039
But to show you that you don't need a GPU, I ran a smaller version on a plain four core

508
00:34:00,039 --> 00:34:00,334
CPU.

509
00:34:00,334 --> 00:34:04,983
600 steps of four rows of 512 tokens, about an hour and a half.

510
00:34:07,483 --> 00:34:10,138
Now look at the very first step.

511
00:34:10,138 --> 00:34:11,383
A loss of 11.9.

512
00:34:11,383 --> 00:34:15,033
Why so much higher than in our notebook run?

513
00:34:15,033 --> 00:34:18,103
Look at the number of trained tokens.

514
00:34:18,103 --> 00:34:18,601
Eight.

515
00:34:18,601 --> 00:34:20,675
In the whole first batch!

516
00:34:20,675 --> 00:34:22,047
Pause and predict.

517
00:34:22,047 --> 00:34:27,078
Why would the very first batch have only eight tokens to train on?

518
00:34:27,078 --> 00:34:29,975
Think about what best fit grabs first.

519
00:34:33,975 --> 00:34:34,988
It's best fit.

520
00:34:34,988 --> 00:34:41,428
At the start, the buffer is full, and the longest conversations in it are those long MMLU

521
00:34:41,428 --> 00:34:42,659
reading passages.

522
00:34:42,659 --> 00:34:48,882
So every row is one of them, and each one has exactly two green tokens, the letter and

523
00:34:48,882 --> 00:34:49,895
assistant end.

524
00:34:49,895 --> 00:34:56,408
And assistant end is one of our special tokens, the ones that start out with a loss of 15.

525
00:34:56,408 --> 00:35:01,908
Then in step two there are 428 trained tokens, and the loss is a normal 2.6.

526
00:35:01,908 --> 00:35:07,987
So the loss of a single step can be really misleading, and that's why we look at the

527
00:35:07,987 --> 00:35:12,401
validation loss, which goes down smoothly, from 2.73 to 1.34.

528
00:35:12,401 --> 00:35:14,132
Okay, now the fun part.

529
00:35:14,132 --> 00:35:15,412
Let's talk to it.

530
00:35:15,412 --> 00:35:21,810
Here's a little chat function: it puts the question in a conversation, renders it for

531
00:35:21,810 --> 00:35:25,122
completion, and samples until assistant end.

532
00:35:26,122 --> 00:35:26,667
"Hello!

533
00:35:26,667 --> 00:35:30,322
How can I help you today?" And then it stopped.

534
00:35:30,322 --> 00:35:33,433
All by itself, it emitted assistant end.

535
00:35:33,433 --> 00:35:37,867
Compare that to the base model, which kept going forever.

536
00:35:37,867 --> 00:35:41,522
So the format, the turn taking, that's learned.

537
00:35:41,522 --> 00:35:43,311
Let's ask about France.

538
00:35:44,311 --> 00:35:47,067
It answers directly now, as an assistant.

539
00:35:47,067 --> 00:35:49,286
But then it gets stuck in a loop.

540
00:35:49,286 --> 00:35:55,000
This is a classic failure of greedy decoding with small models: once it has said "the

541
00:35:55,000 --> 00:35:59,504
capital of France" twice, the most likely thing is to say it again.

542
00:35:59,504 --> 00:36:03,067
Sampling with a temperature helps, so let's try that.

543
00:36:04,067 --> 00:36:04,266
Ha!

544
00:36:04,266 --> 00:36:05,131
Look at this.

545
00:36:05,131 --> 00:36:08,055
"We are asked to find the capital of France.

546
00:36:08,055 --> 00:36:13,905
Let me try a manual approach first." And then it spells France, badly, counts the letter

547
00:36:13,905 --> 00:36:16,298
f, and calls python to double check.

548
00:36:16,298 --> 00:36:20,486
It's running the SpellingBee procedure on a geography question!

549
00:36:20,486 --> 00:36:20,752
Why?

550
00:36:20,752 --> 00:36:22,148
Remember the mixture.

551
00:36:22,148 --> 00:36:26,402
SpellingBee is more than a quarter of everything this model saw.

552
00:36:26,402 --> 00:36:32,186
And a lot of SpellingBee questions are short questions, like "what is the count of e in

553
00:36:32,186 --> 00:36:32,917
some word".

554
00:36:32,917 --> 00:36:38,766
So "what is the capital of France" looks like one of those on the surface, and the model

555
00:36:38,766 --> 00:36:40,827
pattern matches on the surface.

556
00:36:40,827 --> 00:36:46,610
If I sample answers to thirty real questions from the SmolTalk test set, only two start

557
00:36:46,610 --> 00:36:49,402
like this, so it's mostly short questions.

558
00:36:49,402 --> 00:36:50,998
But it's a great lesson.

559
00:36:50,998 --> 00:36:56,914
Every dataset in the mixture shapes the model, in ways that leak out beyond that dataset.

560
00:36:56,914 --> 00:37:02,830
When Karpathy added SpellingBee to nanochat, he noted in the commit that it probably uses

561
00:37:02,830 --> 00:37:04,625
too many of these examples.

562
00:37:04,625 --> 00:37:09,478
And on a GPU, with all of SmolTalk in the mixture, this mostly goes away.

563
00:37:10,478 --> 00:37:14,814
And this one is kind of hilarious, meteoroids and molten metal.

564
00:37:14,814 --> 00:37:17,430
So let's be honest about what we have.

565
00:37:17,430 --> 00:37:23,349
This is a 124 million parameter model from 2019, finetuned for an hour and a half on a

566
00:37:23,349 --> 00:37:23,625
CPU.

567
00:37:23,625 --> 00:37:29,131
It doesn't know why the sky is blue, and it's happy to make things up with total

568
00:37:29,131 --> 00:37:29,888
confidence.

569
00:37:29,888 --> 00:37:35,051
The nanochat README says talking to its speedrun model is like talking to a

570
00:37:35,051 --> 00:37:37,942
kindergartener, and we're well below that.

571
00:37:37,942 --> 00:37:43,999
But the format is there, the turn taking is there, and as we'll see, on the narrow tasks

572
00:37:43,999 --> 00:37:47,441
we specifically trained for, it can do quite well.

573
00:37:47,441 --> 00:37:54,624
Exercise: Re-run SFT with five thousand SpellingBee examples instead of twenty thousand,

574
00:37:54,624 --> 00:38:01,643
and measure how often the model starts an ordinary answer with "We are asked to find".

575
00:38:01,643 --> 00:38:04,989
What happens to its SpellingBee accuracy?

576
00:38:04,989 --> 00:38:08,907
And exercise nine is the learning rate schedule.

577
00:38:08,907 --> 00:38:11,682
Pause the video and give it a try.

578
00:38:15,182 --> 00:38:19,492
Okay, so far we've been using the Engine to sample, as a black box.

579
00:38:19,492 --> 00:38:20,714
Now let's write it.

580
00:38:20,714 --> 00:38:25,859
Sampling has two problems that we need to solve: it needs to be fast, because in

581
00:38:25,859 --> 00:38:31,519
reinforcement learning we're going to sample a lot, and it needs to run the python tool.

582
00:38:31,519 --> 00:38:32,999
Let's start with speed.

583
00:38:32,999 --> 00:38:38,144
Here's the simplest sampling loop you could write, the one from the GPT-2 video.

584
00:38:39,144 --> 00:38:41,338
Several seconds for 100 tokens.

585
00:38:41,338 --> 00:38:42,399
Why is it slow?

586
00:38:42,399 --> 00:38:44,663
Think about what this loop does.

587
00:38:44,663 --> 00:38:49,475
To get token number 7, it runs tokens 1 through 6 through the model.

588
00:38:49,475 --> 00:38:55,560
Then to get token 8, it runs tokens 1 through 7 through the model, all of them, again.

589
00:38:55,560 --> 00:38:59,027
But nothing about tokens 1 through 6 has changed!

590
00:38:59,027 --> 00:39:05,113
Because of the causal mask, a token can only look at tokens before it, so its keys and

591
00:39:05,113 --> 00:39:09,075
values in every layer are exactly the same as last time.

592
00:39:09,075 --> 00:39:15,090
We're recomputing them over and over, and the total work grows like the square of the

593
00:39:15,090 --> 00:39:15,585
length.

594
00:39:15,585 --> 00:39:17,702
So the fix is to remember them.

595
00:39:17,702 --> 00:39:19,067
That's the KV cache.

596
00:39:19,067 --> 00:39:24,871
For every layer, we keep the keys and values of all the tokens so far, and when a new

597
00:39:24,871 --> 00:39:30,812
token comes in, we only compute the query, key and value for that one token, append its

598
00:39:30,812 --> 00:39:36,274
key and value to the cache, and let its query attend to everything in the cache.

599
00:39:36,274 --> 00:39:42,283
Now, this needs three small changes inside our GPT, and you should make them in your own

600
00:39:42,283 --> 00:39:44,195
gpt.py from the GPT-2 video.

601
00:39:44,195 --> 00:39:46,312
Change one is the cache itself.

602
00:39:46,312 --> 00:39:52,116
It's a tiny class that holds the keys and values, and update appends the new ones and

603
00:39:52,116 --> 00:39:53,413
returns everything.

604
00:39:53,413 --> 00:39:55,530
Change two is in the attention.

605
00:39:55,530 --> 00:40:00,105
If there's a cache, we append and get back all the keys and values.

606
00:40:00,105 --> 00:40:01,743
And here's a subtle one.

607
00:40:01,743 --> 00:40:03,077
Pause and predict.

608
00:40:03,077 --> 00:40:09,229
When we feed in just one new token, and everything else is in the cache, should the

609
00:40:09,229 --> 00:40:11,304
attention use a causal mask?

610
00:40:15,304 --> 00:40:20,965
If we feed in a single new token, we don't want a causal mask at all, because that one

611
00:40:20,965 --> 00:40:24,388
token is allowed to look at everything in the cache.

612
00:40:24,388 --> 00:40:26,495
And change three, the positions.

613
00:40:26,495 --> 00:40:32,156
The position embedding of the new token must start where the cache left off, otherwise

614
00:40:32,156 --> 00:40:35,448
every new token would think it's at position zero.

615
00:40:35,448 --> 00:40:38,344
And with that, here's sampling with a cache.

616
00:40:39,344 --> 00:40:43,897
First we run the whole prompt through once, which fills the cache.

617
00:40:43,897 --> 00:40:45,691
That's called the prefill.

618
00:40:45,691 --> 00:40:49,485
And then every step, we only feed in the one new token.

619
00:40:49,485 --> 00:40:55,486
Exactly the same tokens as the naive version, which is the first thing to always check,

620
00:40:55,486 --> 00:40:57,418
and about four times faster.

621
00:40:57,418 --> 00:40:59,556
And that gap grows with length.

622
00:40:59,556 --> 00:41:05,696
And here's the cache of the first layer: batch of one, twelve heads, 106 positions, which

623
00:41:05,696 --> 00:41:10,456
is our 6 prompt tokens plus 100 new ones, and 64 dimensions per head.

624
00:41:10,456 --> 00:41:14,393
One more trick, which we'll need for reinforcement learning.

625
00:41:14,393 --> 00:41:17,476
Often we want many samples for the same prompt.

626
00:41:17,476 --> 00:41:23,250
So we do the prefill once, with a batch size of one, and then we just copy the cache for

627
00:41:23,250 --> 00:41:27,383
every sample, and decode all of them in parallel, as one batch.

628
00:41:27,383 --> 00:41:30,012
Okay, now the second job, tools.

629
00:41:30,012 --> 00:41:33,462
The model is sampling one token at a time.

630
00:41:33,462 --> 00:41:39,295
At some point it emits python start, then some tokens, then python end.

631
00:41:39,295 --> 00:41:40,719
Pause and predict.

632
00:41:40,719 --> 00:41:47,602
What should happen when the model emits python end, and what should the model see next?

633
00:41:51,602 --> 00:41:52,751
Here's the idea.

634
00:41:52,751 --> 00:41:56,990
When we sample python start, we start recording the tokens.

635
00:41:56,990 --> 00:42:03,168
When we sample python end, we decode the recorded tokens into a string, and we run it.

636
00:42:03,168 --> 00:42:05,036
And then here's the trick.

637
00:42:05,036 --> 00:42:11,213
We put output start, the tokens of the result, and output end into a queue, and on the

638
00:42:11,213 --> 00:42:17,176
next steps, instead of using what the model sampled, we take tokens from the queue.

639
00:42:17,176 --> 00:42:18,397
We force them in.

640
00:42:18,397 --> 00:42:24,575
So the result appears in the model's own context, as if it had written it, and then it

641
00:42:24,575 --> 00:42:26,443
just continues from there.

642
00:42:26,443 --> 00:42:27,520
Let's write it.

643
00:42:28,520 --> 00:42:34,423
So every step, if there are forced tokens waiting, we take one, otherwise we use the

644
00:42:34,423 --> 00:42:35,478
model's choice.

645
00:42:35,478 --> 00:42:41,662
On python start we start recording, on python end we run it, and we queue up the result.

646
00:42:41,662 --> 00:42:47,002
And let's try it on our SFT model, on a spelling question it has never seen.

647
00:42:47,002 --> 00:42:48,689
So look at what happens.

648
00:42:48,689 --> 00:42:52,273
It spells the word, and it gets the spelling wrong!

649
00:42:52,273 --> 00:42:53,046
e, y, u, r.

650
00:42:53,046 --> 00:42:59,300
It roughly knows which letters are in there, but not in which order, because remember, it

651
00:42:59,300 --> 00:43:00,917
never sees the letters.

652
00:43:00,917 --> 00:43:06,960
Then it counts the u's in its own misspelled version and gets one, which happens to be

653
00:43:06,960 --> 00:43:07,382
right.

654
00:43:07,382 --> 00:43:13,285
Then it writes the python call, and in the python call it copies the word perfectly,

655
00:43:13,285 --> 00:43:15,463
because copying tokens is easy.

656
00:43:15,463 --> 00:43:18,696
And our code runs it and forces in the result.

657
00:43:18,696 --> 00:43:20,664
And the final answer is one.

658
00:43:20,664 --> 00:43:24,318
So the tool is robust to the model's own sloppiness.

659
00:43:24,318 --> 00:43:25,967
Now, a word about safety.

660
00:43:25,967 --> 00:43:31,639
We're running code that a neural network wrote, so the calculator is deliberately very

661
00:43:31,639 --> 00:43:32,364
restricted.

662
00:43:32,364 --> 00:43:38,234
It allows numbers and arithmetic, without powers, or the count method of a string, and it

663
00:43:38,234 --> 00:43:40,279
evaluates without any builtins.

664
00:43:41,279 --> 00:43:47,295
So arithmetic and counting work, and an attempt to run a shell command, or to compute a

665
00:43:47,295 --> 00:43:49,715
gigantic number, just returns None.

666
00:43:49,715 --> 00:43:54,209
Never give a language model an unrestricted eval on your machine.

667
00:43:54,209 --> 00:44:00,014
And the Engine in the repo is exactly what we just wrote, but for a whole batch of samples

668
00:44:00,014 --> 00:44:05,819
at once, with every row keeping its own little state, the prefill once and copy trick, and

669
00:44:05,819 --> 00:44:11,495
one more thing: it returns a mask, one for tokens the model sampled, and zero for tokens

670
00:44:11,495 --> 00:44:12,785
that were forced in.

671
00:44:12,785 --> 00:44:17,236
Remember that mask, because we'll need it for reinforcement learning.

672
00:44:17,236 --> 00:44:20,332
Let's sample eight answers to the same question.

673
00:44:21,332 --> 00:44:27,867
Every sample has three forced tokens, output start, the result, and output end, and they

674
00:44:27,867 --> 00:44:33,139
come out with different lengths, because we sampled with a temperature.

675
00:44:33,139 --> 00:44:39,245
Exercise: Add the three KV cache changes to your own GPT from the GPT-2 video, and check

676
00:44:39,245 --> 00:44:44,379
that your cached sampling gives exactly the same tokens as naive sampling.

677
00:44:44,379 --> 00:44:50,277
Then make the cache faster: instead of concatenating tensors every step, allocate the

678
00:44:50,277 --> 00:44:52,983
whole cache up front and write into it.

679
00:44:52,983 --> 00:44:58,811
And exercises ten to thirteen in the notebook take you through the whole engine: the

680
00:44:58,811 --> 00:45:04,847
cache, cached sampling, the safe calculator, and tool use, checked against a fake model

681
00:45:04,847 --> 00:45:07,900
that tries to hallucinate the tool's output.

682
00:45:07,900 --> 00:45:10,259
Pause the video and give it a try.

683
00:45:13,759 --> 00:45:17,491
Okay, so we have a model that seems to work.

684
00:45:17,491 --> 00:45:22,240
But "seems to work" is not good enough, we need numbers.

685
00:45:22,240 --> 00:45:26,481
And there are two kinds of evaluation in nanochat.

686
00:45:26,481 --> 00:45:28,952
The first is multiple choice, like MMLU.

687
00:45:28,952 --> 00:45:32,287
And here's a neat thing: we don't even have to sample.

688
00:45:32,287 --> 00:45:36,611
We render the question, prime the assistant, and run one forward pass.

689
00:45:36,611 --> 00:45:42,047
Then we look at the logits at the last position, which is the model's prediction for the

690
00:45:42,047 --> 00:45:47,483
first token of its answer, but only at the logits of the allowed letters, A, B, C and D.

691
00:45:47,483 --> 00:45:49,953
Whichever is biggest, that's the answer.

692
00:45:49,953 --> 00:45:54,771
Let's write it, and compare the base model and our SFT model on 100 questions.

693
00:45:54,771 --> 00:45:56,450
Pause and predict.

694
00:45:56,450 --> 00:45:58,875
Four choices per question.

695
00:45:58,875 --> 00:46:04,750
What accuracy do you expect from GPT-2, and from our SFT model?

696
00:46:09,750 --> 00:46:15,243
One forward pass, the logits at the last position, restricted to the letters.

697
00:46:15,243 --> 00:46:21,021
And both are about 25 percent, which is exactly random guessing for four choices.

698
00:46:21,021 --> 00:46:26,941
And with only 100 questions, the noise on these numbers is about plus or minus five

699
00:46:26,941 --> 00:46:30,508
percent, so 29 versus 26 is not a real difference.

700
00:46:30,508 --> 00:46:33,076
Now this is important to understand.

701
00:46:33,076 --> 00:46:37,570
GPT-2 small simply doesn't know the answers to these questions.

702
00:46:37,570 --> 00:46:42,563
SFT can teach a format, but it can't teach knowledge that isn't there.

703
00:46:42,563 --> 00:46:48,769
For MMLU to move, you need a much stronger pretrained model, which is a big part of why

704
00:46:48,769 --> 00:46:51,337
nanochat trains its own base models.

705
00:46:51,337 --> 00:46:53,516
The second kind is generative.

706
00:46:53,516 --> 00:46:58,964
We sample an answer, pull out the number after the hash signs, and compare.

707
00:46:58,964 --> 00:47:03,249
Let's do 20 SpellingBee questions the model has never seen.

708
00:47:04,249 --> 00:47:05,649
Nine out of twenty.

709
00:47:05,649 --> 00:47:06,680
So about half.

710
00:47:06,680 --> 00:47:12,720
That's a narrow, procedural task, and SFT taught the model a procedure that it can

711
00:47:12,720 --> 00:47:15,299
actually execute, most of the time.

712
00:47:15,299 --> 00:47:21,929
In the repo, chat_eval.py has both of these, plus something nanochat calls ChatCORE: every

713
00:47:21,929 --> 00:47:28,338
accuracy is rescaled so that zero means random guessing and one means perfect, and then

714
00:47:28,338 --> 00:47:30,179
averaged into one number.

715
00:47:30,179 --> 00:47:33,715
Here's what I got on 100 problems of every task.

716
00:47:36,215 --> 00:47:39,768
Multiple choice is at chance for both, as we saw.

717
00:47:39,768 --> 00:47:46,150
GSM8K is zero for both: the SFT model learned what a math solution looks like, with tool

718
00:47:46,150 --> 00:47:49,920
calls and hash signs, but it can't do the reasoning.

719
00:47:49,920 --> 00:47:53,183
And SpellingBee goes from zero to 53 percent.

720
00:47:53,183 --> 00:47:58,840
So the ChatCORE goes from 0.02 to 0.11, and almost all of that is SpellingBee.

721
00:47:58,840 --> 00:48:05,366
And 53 percent is not great, but it's not zero either, and that's exactly where we want to

722
00:48:05,366 --> 00:48:06,961
be for the next stage.

723
00:48:06,961 --> 00:48:14,461
Exercise: Evaluate the base model on MMLU with a few-shot prompt, using plain text instead

724
00:48:14,461 --> 00:48:16,044
of our chat format.

725
00:48:16,044 --> 00:48:20,794
How close can you get to the SFT model just by prompting?

726
00:48:20,794 --> 00:48:23,627
Pause the video and give it a try.

727
00:48:27,127 --> 00:48:31,712
Okay, and now the last stage, and I think the most exciting one.

728
00:48:31,712 --> 00:48:33,002
Why do we need it?

729
00:48:33,002 --> 00:48:35,079
Karpathy has a great analogy.

730
00:48:35,079 --> 00:48:36,512
Think of a textbook.

731
00:48:36,512 --> 00:48:39,879
Most of it is exposition, background knowledge.

732
00:48:39,879 --> 00:48:41,240
That's pretraining.

733
00:48:41,240 --> 00:48:47,402
Then there are worked examples, where an expert solves a problem step by step, and you

734
00:48:47,402 --> 00:48:48,333
imitate them.

735
00:48:48,333 --> 00:48:49,121
That's SFT.

736
00:48:49,121 --> 00:48:55,569
And at the end of every chapter, there are practice problems, with the final answer in the

737
00:48:55,569 --> 00:48:57,933
back of the book but no solution.

738
00:48:57,933 --> 00:49:03,091
You try, you fail, you try again, and you figure out what works for you.

739
00:49:03,091 --> 00:49:05,240
That's reinforcement learning.

740
00:49:05,240 --> 00:49:08,195
And here's the deep reason why this matters.

741
00:49:08,195 --> 00:49:13,903
With SFT, we make the model imitate a solution that some person, or some other model,

742
00:49:13,903 --> 00:49:14,306
wrote.

743
00:49:14,306 --> 00:49:17,194
But that author has a very different brain.

744
00:49:17,194 --> 00:49:22,970
A step that's trivial for them might be a leap that our little model can't make in one

745
00:49:22,970 --> 00:49:23,372
token.

746
00:49:23,372 --> 00:49:27,805
And a step they wrote out carefully might be obvious to our model.

747
00:49:27,805 --> 00:49:32,976
We don't actually know the best sequence of tokens for this particular model.

748
00:49:32,976 --> 00:49:35,394
So the idea of RL is: don't tell it.

749
00:49:35,394 --> 00:49:39,356
Let it try many times, and whatever works, do more of that.

750
00:49:39,356 --> 00:49:41,364
So let's make that precise.

751
00:49:41,364 --> 00:49:48,057
We have a question, the model samples an answer y, and we check it with our grader and get

752
00:49:48,057 --> 00:49:51,626
a reward R, one if it's correct and zero if not.

753
00:49:51,626 --> 00:49:58,170
And we want to maximize the expected reward, the average reward of the answers the model

754
00:49:58,170 --> 00:49:59,137
would sample.

755
00:49:59,137 --> 00:50:03,301
Now how do we get a gradient of an average over samples?

756
00:50:03,301 --> 00:50:06,796
Here's the trick, and it's worth deriving once.

757
00:50:06,796 --> 00:50:13,266
The expected reward is a sum over all possible answers of the probability of the answer

758
00:50:13,266 --> 00:50:19,513
times its reward, so its gradient is the sum of the reward times the gradient of the

759
00:50:19,513 --> 00:50:20,405
probability.

760
00:50:20,405 --> 00:50:27,024
Now the gradient of p is the same thing as p times the gradient of log p, that's just the

761
00:50:27,024 --> 00:50:28,585
chain rule backwards.

762
00:50:28,585 --> 00:50:32,304
And plugging that in, we get an expectation again!

763
00:50:32,304 --> 00:50:38,104
The average, over sampled answers, of the reward times the gradient of the log

764
00:50:38,104 --> 00:50:38,996
probability.

765
00:50:38,996 --> 00:50:42,492
And an expectation we can estimate by sampling.

766
00:50:42,492 --> 00:50:48,961
And the log probability of an answer is the sum of the log probabilities of its tokens,

767
00:50:48,961 --> 00:50:53,126
which is just minus the loss our model already computes.

768
00:50:53,126 --> 00:50:56,536
So in code, it's almost embarrassingly simple.

769
00:50:56,536 --> 00:51:03,209
Sample some answers, and for each one, take the log probability of its tokens, multiply by

770
00:51:03,209 --> 00:51:06,397
its reward, and do gradient ascent on that.

771
00:51:06,397 --> 00:51:12,402
If you squint, it's just SFT on your own samples, weighted by how good they were.

772
00:51:12,402 --> 00:51:16,035
This is called the policy gradient, or REINFORCE.

773
00:51:16,035 --> 00:51:17,490
But there's one problem.

774
00:51:17,490 --> 00:51:22,641
If rewards are zero or one, the wrong answers get a weight of zero, so they're simply

775
00:51:22,641 --> 00:51:23,126
ignored.

776
00:51:23,126 --> 00:51:28,580
And on easy questions, where every answer is right, everything gets pushed up, even though

777
00:51:28,580 --> 00:51:30,095
there's nothing to learn.

778
00:51:30,095 --> 00:51:32,338
So what we do is subtract a baseline.

779
00:51:32,338 --> 00:51:37,731
For every question, we sample a group of answers, say eight of them, and the advantage of

780
00:51:37,731 --> 00:51:41,428
each answer is its reward minus the mean reward of the group.

781
00:51:41,428 --> 00:51:46,701
So an answer that's better than average for this question has a positive advantage, and

782
00:51:46,701 --> 00:51:48,579
its probability gets pushed up.

783
00:51:48,579 --> 00:51:51,792
An answer that's worse than average gets pushed down.

784
00:51:51,792 --> 00:51:57,185
And you can show that subtracting a baseline doesn't change the expected gradient at all,

785
00:51:57,185 --> 00:51:59,246
it just makes it a lot less noisy.

786
00:51:59,246 --> 00:52:03,003
This is the group in GRPO, group relative policy optimization.

787
00:52:03,003 --> 00:52:09,074
Before we do this on a transformer, let's build intuition on the tiniest possible example.

788
00:52:09,074 --> 00:52:14,740
Imagine a language model that produces a single token, out of four options, and only

789
00:52:14,740 --> 00:52:16,224
option two is correct.

790
00:52:16,224 --> 00:52:18,315
The whole model is four logits.

791
00:52:18,315 --> 00:52:24,183
We sample a group of sixteen attempts, reward the correct ones, compute advantages, and

792
00:52:24,183 --> 00:52:25,599
take a gradient step.

793
00:52:26,599 --> 00:52:32,831
The probability of the correct answer starts at a quarter, because all logits are zero,

794
00:52:32,831 --> 00:52:37,846
after ten steps it's at 85 percent, and it keeps climbing towards one.

795
00:52:37,846 --> 00:52:40,066
Nobody ever told it the answer.

796
00:52:40,066 --> 00:52:43,576
It just tried things and did more of what worked.

797
00:52:43,576 --> 00:52:49,665
Now what if the model starts out really bad, and the correct answer is very unlikely?

798
00:52:49,665 --> 00:52:51,274
Pause and predict.

799
00:52:51,274 --> 00:52:59,140
The correct answer starts with a logit of minus ten, a probability of about one in sixty

800
00:52:59,140 --> 00:52:59,945
thousand.

801
00:52:59,945 --> 00:53:01,107
What happens?

802
00:53:06,107 --> 00:53:07,172
Nothing happens.

803
00:53:07,172 --> 00:53:13,033
The correct answer is never sampled, so the reward is always zero, so every advantage is

804
00:53:13,033 --> 00:53:15,030
zero, and there's no gradient.

805
00:53:15,030 --> 00:53:20,624
And this is the most important lesson about RL: it can only amplify things the model

806
00:53:20,624 --> 00:53:22,156
already does sometimes.

807
00:53:22,156 --> 00:53:24,819
It can't create a behavior from nothing.

808
00:53:24,819 --> 00:53:30,413
That's why we do SFT first, to get into a regime where the model sometimes succeeds.

809
00:53:30,413 --> 00:53:36,340
And the same is true at the other end: if all eight answers are right, every advantage is

810
00:53:36,340 --> 00:53:36,939
zero too.

811
00:53:36,939 --> 00:53:41,268
RL only learns from questions at the edge of the model's ability.

812
00:53:41,268 --> 00:53:44,451
Okay, now the real thing, on our SFT model.

813
00:53:44,451 --> 00:53:45,858
Step one, rollouts.

814
00:53:45,858 --> 00:53:52,373
We take a SpellingBee question, render it for completion, and use our engine to sample a

815
00:53:52,373 --> 00:53:57,407
group of eight answers, at temperature one, because we want variety.

816
00:53:57,407 --> 00:53:59,109
Then we grade each one.

817
00:54:00,109 --> 00:54:01,803
How many o's in spook.

818
00:54:01,803 --> 00:54:03,574
Four right, four wrong.

819
00:54:03,574 --> 00:54:08,040
That's a question right at the edge, perfect for learning.

820
00:54:08,040 --> 00:54:10,658
Now step two, the policy gradient.

821
00:54:10,658 --> 00:54:17,433
We pad the samples to the same length, and build inputs and targets exactly like in SFT.

822
00:54:17,433 --> 00:54:24,132
And here's where the mask from the engine comes in: we set the targets to minus one for

823
00:54:24,132 --> 00:54:29,676
the prompt, for the padding, and for the tokens that the tool forced in.

824
00:54:29,676 --> 00:54:34,141
We only train on the tokens that the model actually chose.

825
00:54:34,141 --> 00:54:38,222
Then advantages, the log probabilities, and the loss.

826
00:54:39,222 --> 00:54:40,418
There's the mask.

827
00:54:40,418 --> 00:54:45,482
The advantages are plus or minus a half, exactly like on the whiteboard.

828
00:54:45,482 --> 00:54:51,812
The model gives us the loss of every token, the negative log probability, so we negate it,

829
00:54:51,812 --> 00:54:55,188
and the ignored tokens come out as exactly zero.

830
00:54:55,188 --> 00:54:58,985
And here's the average log probability of each answer.

831
00:54:58,985 --> 00:55:05,315
The fifth answer, a wrong one, has a much lower log probability per token than the others,

832
00:55:05,315 --> 00:55:08,621
it's a bit of an unusual sample for this model.

833
00:55:08,621 --> 00:55:14,740
Then the loss: every token's log probability times the advantage of its answer, summed,

834
00:55:14,740 --> 00:55:21,000
divided by the number of trained tokens, and with a minus sign, because PyTorch minimizes

835
00:55:21,000 --> 00:55:22,688
and we want to maximize.

836
00:55:22,688 --> 00:55:25,571
And then backward, and an optimizer step.

837
00:55:25,571 --> 00:55:27,470
That's the whole algorithm.

838
00:55:27,470 --> 00:55:32,582
Now if you've read about GRPO or PPO, you may wonder where everything else is.

839
00:55:32,582 --> 00:55:38,415
And nanochat makes four deliberate simplifications, written right at the top of the file.

840
00:55:38,415 --> 00:55:41,757
Number one, there's no KL penalty to the SFT model.

841
00:55:41,757 --> 00:55:47,590
That's a regularizer that keeps the model from drifting too far, and for a short run like

842
00:55:47,590 --> 00:55:48,966
ours it's not needed.

843
00:55:48,966 --> 00:55:51,457
Number two, no PPO ratio and clipping.

844
00:55:51,457 --> 00:55:57,290
Those exist because PPO takes several gradient steps on the same batch of samples, so the

845
00:55:57,290 --> 00:56:00,567
samples go stale and you have to correct for that.

846
00:56:00,567 --> 00:56:06,399
We take exactly one step per batch, so we're always on-policy, and the ratio would always

847
00:56:06,399 --> 00:56:07,382
be exactly one.

848
00:56:07,382 --> 00:56:13,150
Number three, we divide by the total number of tokens, not per answer, as the DAPO paper

849
00:56:13,150 --> 00:56:16,230
suggests, so long answers aren't down-weighted.

850
00:56:16,230 --> 00:56:21,014
And number four, we don't divide the advantage by the standard deviation.

851
00:56:21,014 --> 00:56:26,716
Put it all together, and what's left is good old REINFORCE, with a group mean baseline.

852
00:56:26,716 --> 00:56:33,283
And chat_rl.py is exactly the two cells we just wrote, the rollouts, and the loss, plus an

853
00:56:33,283 --> 00:56:36,858
evaluation on held out questions every few steps.

854
00:56:36,858 --> 00:56:42,039
On a GPU you'd use sixteen questions per step and sixteen samples each.

855
00:56:42,039 --> 00:56:48,314
On my CPU, I did 40 steps of eight questions with eight samples each, which took about

856
00:56:48,314 --> 00:56:50,722
three hours, most of it sampling.

857
00:56:50,722 --> 00:56:51,966
Pause and predict.

858
00:56:51,966 --> 00:56:58,113
We measure pass at one, the fraction of single samples that are right, and pass at eight,

859
00:56:58,113 --> 00:57:02,949
the fraction of questions with at least one right answer out of eight.

860
00:57:02,949 --> 00:57:05,090
Which one will RL improve more?

861
00:57:11,590 --> 00:57:13,015
So let's read this.

862
00:57:13,015 --> 00:57:19,463
Pass at one is the fraction of single samples that are correct, and before RL, it's 44

863
00:57:19,463 --> 00:57:20,063
percent.

864
00:57:20,063 --> 00:57:26,812
Pass at eight is the fraction of questions where at least one of eight samples is correct,

865
00:57:26,812 --> 00:57:28,312
and it's 92 percent.

866
00:57:28,312 --> 00:57:33,936
So the model can already solve almost every question, it's just unreliable.

867
00:57:33,936 --> 00:57:40,310
And after only ten steps of RL, pass at one jumps to 84 percent, and ends at 87 and a

868
00:57:40,310 --> 00:57:40,685
half.

869
00:57:40,685 --> 00:57:43,084
This is a really common pattern.

870
00:57:43,084 --> 00:57:49,458
RL takes what the model can do some of the time, and makes it do it most of the time.

871
00:57:49,458 --> 00:57:52,158
It turns pass at k into pass at one.

872
00:57:52,158 --> 00:57:56,440
And the training reward is really noisy, look at step 26.

873
00:57:56,440 --> 00:58:03,127
That's because every step only has eight questions, and some steps just get harder words.

874
00:58:03,127 --> 00:58:07,635
Always trust a held out evaluation, not the training reward.

875
00:58:07,635 --> 00:58:09,363
And one more fun thing.

876
00:58:09,363 --> 00:58:13,870
The answers get shorter, from about 210 tokens to about 155.

877
00:58:13,870 --> 00:58:15,523
Nobody asked for that.

878
00:58:15,523 --> 00:58:22,135
But many long answers were ones where the model got lost, misspelling a word into a long

879
00:58:22,135 --> 00:58:27,920
string of garbage letters, and those got zero reward, so RL pushed them down.

880
00:58:27,920 --> 00:58:34,681
And with greedy decoding on 100 test questions, SpellingBee goes from 53 percent after SFT

881
00:58:34,681 --> 00:58:36,409
to 86 percent after RL.

882
00:58:36,409 --> 00:58:39,582
So now, finally, the famous question.

883
00:58:39,582 --> 00:58:46,440
Eight samples from the SFT model and from the RL model, and their final answers.

884
00:58:47,440 --> 00:58:52,181
Before RL, three r's in strawberry only once out of eight.

885
00:58:52,181 --> 00:58:54,552
After RL, eight out of eight.

886
00:58:54,552 --> 00:58:55,860
Same for cheese.

887
00:58:55,860 --> 00:58:56,677
That's RL.

888
00:58:56,677 --> 00:59:02,644
But look at the last question, without the quotes and without "the word".

889
00:59:02,644 --> 00:59:04,524
Both models fall apart.

890
00:59:04,524 --> 00:59:05,941
Pause and predict.

891
00:59:05,941 --> 00:59:11,688
With quotes, the RL model gets strawberry right eight times out of eight.

892
00:59:11,688 --> 00:59:13,971
Without them, it falls apart.

893
00:59:13,971 --> 00:59:14,286
Why?

894
00:59:14,286 --> 00:59:16,253
Hint: think about tokens.

895
00:59:20,253 --> 00:59:21,690
Let's look.

896
00:59:22,690 --> 00:59:27,150
It says: we are asked to find the number of r's in the word "sweet".

897
00:59:27,150 --> 00:59:27,544
Sweet!

898
00:59:27,544 --> 00:59:29,839
It didn't even copy the right word.

899
00:59:29,839 --> 00:59:31,676
And the tokens tell you why.

900
00:59:31,676 --> 00:59:35,939
Without quotes, the word is "space strawberry", one single token.

901
00:59:35,939 --> 00:59:41,711
With quotes, it's "st", "raw", "berry", exactly the tokens the model has to write in its

902
00:59:41,711 --> 00:59:43,810
spelling and in the python call.

903
00:59:43,810 --> 00:59:47,024
So with quotes, it just has to copy three tokens.

904
00:59:47,024 --> 00:59:52,337
Without quotes, it has to know how one single token is spelled, and write it as a

905
00:59:52,337 --> 00:59:58,109
different set of tokens, and it doesn't know that, so it reaches for something nearby in

906
00:59:58,109 --> 00:59:59,092
meaning: sweet.

907
00:59:59,092 --> 01:00:04,799
Our SpellingBee data mostly has rare words, which are several tokens either way, so the

908
01:00:04,799 --> 01:00:08,537
model never learned to unpack a common single-token word.

909
01:00:08,537 --> 01:00:10,243
The tokenizer, once again.

910
01:00:10,243 --> 01:00:16,146
And the fix is what you'd do in practice: now that we understand the failure, add training

911
01:00:16,146 --> 01:00:17,917
data for exactly this case.

912
01:00:17,917 --> 01:00:23,164
You look at the failures, you understand them, and you go back and fix the data.

913
01:00:23,164 --> 01:00:29,687
Exercise: Write the RL training loop yourself, using the two cells above: a loop over

914
01:00:29,687 --> 01:00:36,211
questions, an AdamW optimizer with a small learning rate, and an evaluation of pass@1

915
01:00:36,211 --> 01:00:37,439
every ten steps.

916
01:00:37,439 --> 01:00:44,193
Then try changing the reward, for example a small penalty for long answers, and see what

917
01:00:44,193 --> 01:00:45,345
the model does.

918
01:00:45,345 --> 01:00:49,182
Careful: RL will find any loophole in your reward.

919
01:00:49,182 --> 01:00:56,013
Exercises fourteen to sixteen in the notebook are the pieces you need: pass at k, the log

920
01:00:56,013 --> 01:01:00,234
probability of an answer, and the policy gradient loss.

921
01:01:00,234 --> 01:01:02,844
Pause the video and give it a try.

922
01:01:06,344 --> 01:01:09,994
Okay, so RL works great when we can check the answer.

923
01:01:09,994 --> 01:01:13,644
But remember the problem we found in the SFT chapter?

924
01:01:13,644 --> 01:01:19,430
On ordinary questions, our model sometimes starts running the SpellingBee procedure.

925
01:01:19,430 --> 01:01:23,562
Let's measure how often, for the SFT model and the RL model.

926
01:01:23,562 --> 01:01:29,692
I'm taking twenty ordinary questions from the SmolTalk test set, nothing about letters or

927
01:01:29,692 --> 01:01:35,408
counting, sampling four answers to each, and checking whether the answer starts the

928
01:01:35,408 --> 01:01:36,097
procedure.

929
01:01:37,097 --> 01:01:39,815
One in eight answers, for both models.

930
01:01:39,815 --> 01:01:45,967
So this habit came from the SFT data mixture, and RL didn't fix it, of course: RL only

931
01:01:45,967 --> 01:01:52,404
ever practiced on spelling questions, so on ordinary questions, nobody ever told the model

932
01:01:52,404 --> 01:01:53,763
that this is wrong.

933
01:01:53,763 --> 01:01:55,051
So how do we fix it?

934
01:01:55,051 --> 01:02:00,779
Honestly, the best fix is in the data: use fewer SpellingBee examples in the SFT mixture,

935
01:02:00,779 --> 01:02:03,547
which is the exercise from the SFT chapter.

936
01:02:03,547 --> 01:02:07,537
But I want to use this as an example of a much bigger problem.

937
01:02:07,537 --> 01:02:12,300
Most of the things we want from an assistant, we can't write a grader for.

938
01:02:12,300 --> 01:02:13,781
Is this answer helpful?

939
01:02:13,781 --> 01:02:14,746
Is it too long?

940
01:02:14,746 --> 01:02:15,454
Is it rude?

941
01:02:15,454 --> 01:02:16,677
Is this joke funny?

942
01:02:16,677 --> 01:02:19,638
There's no answer key in the back of the book.

943
01:02:19,638 --> 01:02:25,367
But there's one thing people are really good at: looking at two answers, and saying which

944
01:02:25,367 --> 01:02:26,268
one is better.

945
01:02:26,268 --> 01:02:28,327
And that's how ChatGPT was made.

946
01:02:28,327 --> 01:02:33,798
In the InstructGPT paper, people compared answers of the model, then OpenAI trained a

947
01:02:33,798 --> 01:02:39,398
reward model, a neural net that predicts which answer people will prefer, and then they

948
01:02:39,398 --> 01:02:44,869
ran RL against that reward model, with a leash that keeps the model close to where it

949
01:02:44,869 --> 01:02:45,384
started.

950
01:02:45,384 --> 01:02:51,113
And in 2023, the DPO paper showed that you can skip the reward model, and the RL, and fit

951
01:02:51,113 --> 01:02:55,168
the model directly to the pairs, with the exact same objective.

952
01:02:55,168 --> 01:02:57,099
Let's see how that's possible.

953
01:02:57,099 --> 01:03:00,767
It's only a few lines of math, and it's really beautiful.

954
01:03:00,767 --> 01:03:03,279
First, we need a model of preferences.

955
01:03:03,279 --> 01:03:06,452
Say every answer has a hidden score, a reward r.

956
01:03:06,452 --> 01:03:12,269
Then the probability that people prefer A over B is the sigmoid of the difference of the

957
01:03:12,269 --> 01:03:12,797
rewards.

958
01:03:12,797 --> 01:03:18,350
If A is a lot better, that's close to one, and if they're equally good, it's a half.

959
01:03:18,350 --> 01:03:20,002
Second, the goal of RLHF.

960
01:03:20,002 --> 01:03:25,951
We want a policy pi, that's our model, with a high expected reward, but we want it to stay

961
01:03:25,951 --> 01:03:31,834
close to the reference model, the model we started from, measured with the KL divergence.

962
01:03:31,834 --> 01:03:34,279
And beta says how tight the leash is.

963
01:03:34,279 --> 01:03:39,898
Without the leash, the model would run off and find whatever weird text maximizes the

964
01:03:39,898 --> 01:03:40,360
reward.

965
01:03:40,360 --> 01:03:43,930
Now it turns out this objective has an exact solution.

966
01:03:43,930 --> 01:03:49,747
The best policy is the reference model, reweighted by the exponential of the reward over

967
01:03:49,747 --> 01:03:54,638
beta, and divided by a normalizer Z, so that the probabilities sum to one.

968
01:03:54,638 --> 01:03:59,133
Z is a sum over every possible answer, so we could never compute it.

969
01:03:59,133 --> 01:04:00,653
But watch what happens.

970
01:04:00,653 --> 01:04:04,156
Take the log of both sides, and solve for the reward.

971
01:04:04,156 --> 01:04:10,039
The reward of an answer is beta times the log ratio of the policy and the reference, plus

972
01:04:10,039 --> 01:04:10,964
that constant.

973
01:04:10,964 --> 01:04:13,410
And now plug that into Bradley-Terry.

974
01:04:13,410 --> 01:04:19,293
We only ever need the difference of two rewards, for two answers to the same question, so

975
01:04:19,293 --> 01:04:21,672
the impossible Z simply cancels out.

976
01:04:21,672 --> 01:04:27,291
What's left is a loss that only needs the log probabilities of two answers, under our

977
01:04:27,291 --> 01:04:29,802
model, and under the frozen reference.

978
01:04:29,802 --> 01:04:33,041
No reward model, and no sampling during training.

979
01:04:33,041 --> 01:04:38,924
It's a classification loss on pairs: make the chosen answer more likely than the rejected

980
01:04:38,924 --> 01:04:40,973
one, relative to the reference.

981
01:04:40,973 --> 01:04:44,258
Let's build intuition on our tiny toy again.

982
01:04:44,258 --> 01:04:50,752
Four possible answers, the reference likes all of them equally, and the labelers prefer

983
01:04:50,752 --> 01:04:53,739
answer two over each of the other three.

984
01:04:53,739 --> 01:04:57,770
So that's three pairs, and we train with the DPO loss.

985
01:04:58,770 --> 01:05:01,716
The loss starts at 0.693, which is log 2.

986
01:05:01,716 --> 01:05:08,038
The policy is the reference, so every margin is zero, and the sigmoid of zero is a half.

987
01:05:08,038 --> 01:05:12,205
Then the probability of answer two goes up, to almost one.

988
01:05:12,205 --> 01:05:14,433
And look at this weight column.

989
01:05:14,433 --> 01:05:20,755
It's the sigmoid of minus the margin, and if you take the gradient of the DPO loss, this

990
01:05:20,755 --> 01:05:26,862
is exactly what multiplies everything: beta, times this weight, times push the chosen

991
01:05:26,862 --> 01:05:29,664
answer up and the rejected answer down.

992
01:05:29,664 --> 01:05:35,915
So it's a lot like REINFORCE with a plus one and a minus one, except that the pairs the

993
01:05:35,915 --> 01:05:38,717
model already gets right stop counting.

994
01:05:38,717 --> 01:05:40,801
Now let's see what beta does.

995
01:05:41,801 --> 01:05:47,381
With a beta of 2, the loss is already happy at 89 percent, and the weight is basically

996
01:05:47,381 --> 01:05:49,068
zero, so it stops pushing.

997
01:05:49,068 --> 01:05:54,324
With a big beta, a small move away from the reference already makes a big margin.

998
01:05:54,324 --> 01:05:55,686
So beta is the leash.

999
01:05:55,686 --> 01:06:01,461
A big beta keeps you close to where you started, and a small beta lets the model run far.

1000
01:06:01,461 --> 01:06:03,927
Remember that, we're going to need it.

1001
01:06:03,927 --> 01:06:05,612
Okay, now the real thing.

1002
01:06:05,612 --> 01:06:06,960
First we need pairs.

1003
01:06:06,960 --> 01:06:13,026
We sample eight answers from the RL model to an ordinary question, and then someone has to

1004
01:06:13,026 --> 01:06:13,768
judge them.

1005
01:06:13,768 --> 01:06:19,835
In real life, this is where a person, or a big LLM acting as a judge, looks at two answers

1006
01:06:19,835 --> 01:06:20,778
and picks one.

1007
01:06:20,778 --> 01:06:26,643
For us, the judge is that regular expression: does the answer start spelling things out

1008
01:06:26,643 --> 01:06:27,856
when nobody asked?

1009
01:06:27,856 --> 01:06:33,720
That's a stand-in, and to be honest, it's so simple that we could have used it as an RL

1010
01:06:33,720 --> 01:06:34,192
reward.

1011
01:06:34,192 --> 01:06:40,057
But it means that you can reproduce every number here, and the DPO code doesn't care at

1012
01:06:40,057 --> 01:06:42,483
all where the preferences came from.

1013
01:06:43,483 --> 01:06:45,077
Ha, look at this one.

1014
01:06:45,077 --> 01:06:51,604
We are asked to find the number of r's in the word "loop quantum gravity transitions".

1015
01:06:51,604 --> 01:06:58,359
The chosen answers aren't exactly good physics, it's a tiny model, but they are the right

1016
01:06:58,359 --> 01:06:59,497
kind of answer.

1017
01:06:59,497 --> 01:07:04,430
So we take one chosen and one rejected answer, and that's a pair.

1018
01:07:04,430 --> 01:07:05,417
Now the loss.

1019
01:07:05,417 --> 01:07:12,096
We need the log probability of a whole answer, which is the sum of the log probabilities

1020
01:07:12,096 --> 01:07:15,967
of its tokens, only over the answer, with our mask.

1021
01:07:15,967 --> 01:07:18,775
And then the DPO loss is three lines.

1022
01:07:19,775 --> 01:07:21,127
Pause and predict.

1023
01:07:21,127 --> 01:07:26,311
Before any training, the policy and the reference are the same model.

1024
01:07:26,311 --> 01:07:28,264
What will the DPO loss be?

1025
01:07:32,264 --> 01:07:33,714
0.693, log 2 again.

1026
01:07:33,714 --> 01:07:40,580
At the start the policy is the reference, so both ratios are zero, the margin is zero, and

1027
01:07:40,580 --> 01:07:43,250
minus log sigmoid of zero is log 2.

1028
01:07:43,250 --> 01:07:50,116
That's our sanity check, just like the 10.8 at initialization in pretraining: if you don't

1029
01:07:50,116 --> 01:07:54,999
get exactly 0.693 at step zero, something is wrong in your code.

1030
01:07:54,999 --> 01:08:01,483
And notice that the chosen answer is much less likely than the rejected one, minus 79

1031
01:08:01,483 --> 01:08:02,704
versus minus 18.

1032
01:08:02,704 --> 01:08:04,916
It's partly that it's longer.

1033
01:08:04,916 --> 01:08:11,630
But it doesn't matter, DPO doesn't look at the raw probabilities, only at how the ratios

1034
01:08:11,630 --> 01:08:13,460
to the reference change.

1035
01:08:13,460 --> 01:08:14,452
Now one step.

1036
01:08:15,452 --> 01:08:21,597
After one step, the chosen answer is e to the 8 times more likely than under the

1037
01:08:21,597 --> 01:08:28,434
reference, the rejected one e to the 15 times less likely, and the loss went from 0.69 to

1038
01:08:28,434 --> 01:08:28,741
0.1.

1039
01:08:28,741 --> 01:08:30,431
One step, on one pair!

1040
01:08:30,431 --> 01:08:34,425
With a single pair, the model can overfit instantly.

1041
01:08:34,425 --> 01:08:40,954
That's why the real run uses hundreds of pairs, a smaller learning rate, and only two

1042
01:08:40,954 --> 01:08:42,567
passes over the data.

1043
01:08:42,567 --> 01:08:45,949
And there's one more thing we must not forget.

1044
01:08:45,949 --> 01:08:52,490
If we only show the model ordinary questions, nothing stops it from learning "never spell

1045
01:08:52,490 --> 01:08:57,121
anything out", and then we'd lose everything RL just taught it.

1046
01:08:57,121 --> 01:09:03,589
So the preference data also has spelling questions, where the chosen answer is a correct

1047
01:09:03,589 --> 01:09:06,824
one, and the rejected answer is a wrong one.

1048
01:09:06,824 --> 01:09:10,793
That's RL's checkable reward, turned into preferences.

1049
01:09:10,793 --> 01:09:12,483
And that's chat_dpo.py.

1050
01:09:12,483 --> 01:09:18,878
The judge for ordinary questions, the judge for spelling questions, and a pair whenever

1051
01:09:18,878 --> 01:09:23,362
there's at least one good and one bad answer among the eight.

1052
01:09:23,362 --> 01:09:29,904
Then, because the reference model never changes, we compute its log probabilities for all

1053
01:09:29,904 --> 01:09:36,519
the pairs once, up front, and the training loop is the same loop as always, with our loss.

1054
01:09:36,519 --> 01:09:42,914
Collecting the pairs is the slow part: 850 questions, eight answers each, about an hour

1055
01:09:42,914 --> 01:09:44,457
and a half on my CPU.

1056
01:09:44,457 --> 01:09:47,765
The training itself is about fifteen minutes.

1057
01:09:47,765 --> 01:09:52,837
Here's plain DPO, exactly the loss we just wrote, with a beta of 0.1.

1058
01:09:52,837 --> 01:09:54,074
Pause and predict.

1059
01:09:54,074 --> 01:10:00,053
On ordinary questions we push the spelling procedure down, and on spelling questions we

1060
01:10:00,053 --> 01:10:01,702
push correct answers up.

1061
01:10:01,702 --> 01:10:06,238
What will happen to the leak, and what will happen to SpellingBee?

1062
01:10:12,738 --> 01:10:15,403
So, 285 pairs out of 850 questions.

1063
01:10:15,403 --> 01:10:21,496
Before DPO, nine percent of the answers to ordinary questions start the spelling

1064
01:10:21,496 --> 01:10:24,847
procedure, and SpellingBee is at 86 percent.

1065
01:10:24,847 --> 01:10:28,426
The loss starts at exactly log 2, like we said.

1066
01:10:28,426 --> 01:10:35,205
The validation accuracy, which is how often the model ranks a held out pair the right way

1067
01:10:35,205 --> 01:10:37,489
around, goes up to 93 percent.

1068
01:10:37,489 --> 01:10:39,089
And the leak is gone.

1069
01:10:39,089 --> 01:10:39,470
Zero.

1070
01:10:39,470 --> 01:10:41,754
DPO did exactly what we asked.

1071
01:10:41,754 --> 01:10:45,334
But SpellingBee crashed, from 86 percent to 47.

1072
01:10:45,334 --> 01:10:48,837
We broke the thing that RL worked so hard for.

1073
01:10:48,837 --> 01:10:50,132
So what happened?

1074
01:10:50,132 --> 01:10:51,655
Look at the rewards.

1075
01:10:51,655 --> 01:10:56,682
The chosen answers barely move, and the rejected ones go way down.

1076
01:10:56,682 --> 01:11:03,307
Remember the implicit reward is beta times the log ratio, so a reward of minus 1.8 at a

1077
01:11:03,307 --> 01:11:09,629
beta of 0.1 means the rejected answers became something like e to the 18 times less

1078
01:11:09,629 --> 01:11:10,162
likely.

1079
01:11:10,162 --> 01:11:14,274
DPO mostly works by pushing the rejected answers down.

1080
01:11:14,274 --> 01:11:16,864
And what are the rejected answers?

1081
01:11:16,864 --> 01:11:20,139
The spelling procedure, in the wrong place.

1082
01:11:20,139 --> 01:11:26,536
The tokens of that procedure get pushed down everywhere, including where it belongs.

1083
01:11:26,536 --> 01:11:33,086
And here's the subtle part: the spelling pairs can't protect it, because in a spelling

1084
01:11:33,086 --> 01:11:39,712
pair, both answers use the procedure, and DPO only ever looks at the difference between

1085
01:11:39,712 --> 01:11:40,321
the two.

1086
01:11:40,321 --> 01:11:43,748
Let's see what it did to a spelling question.

1087
01:11:44,748 --> 01:11:50,235
The RL model spells collarbird badly, as always, and its manual count says one.

1088
01:11:50,235 --> 01:11:54,679
But then it calls python, which saves it, and the answer is two.

1089
01:11:54,679 --> 01:11:57,666
The plain DPO model has lost the procedure.

1090
01:11:57,666 --> 01:12:03,638
It writes something that looks a bit like the python call, but as plain text, it never

1091
01:12:03,638 --> 01:12:08,222
calls the tool, and it repeats itself until it runs out of tokens.

1092
01:12:08,222 --> 01:12:10,583
It drifted away from what it knew.

1093
01:12:10,583 --> 01:12:14,626
So we need two fixes, and both are about not drifting too far.

1094
01:12:14,626 --> 01:12:19,450
The first one is an SFT loss on the chosen answers, added to the DPO loss.

1095
01:12:19,450 --> 01:12:25,318
It's literally the loss from the SFT chapter, the average log probability per token of the

1096
01:12:25,318 --> 01:12:31,055
chosen answer, and it says: whatever you do to the margin, keep the good answers likely.

1097
01:12:31,055 --> 01:12:36,923
And since the chosen spelling answers are correct procedures, that protects the procedure.

1098
01:12:36,923 --> 01:12:39,988
This is what Meta did for Llama 3, for example.

1099
01:12:39,988 --> 01:12:42,074
And the second fix is the leash.

1100
01:12:42,074 --> 01:12:47,876
Remember the toy: with a bigger beta, the loss is satisfied with a smaller move away from

1101
01:12:47,876 --> 01:12:48,789
the reference.

1102
01:12:48,789 --> 01:12:50,419
So we go from 0.1 to 0.5.

1103
01:12:50,419 --> 01:12:53,092
Those are now the defaults of the script.

1104
01:12:53,092 --> 01:12:57,787
The pairs are already saved, so this time it just loads them and trains.

1105
01:13:00,287 --> 01:13:05,289
This time the chosen answers actually go up, the SFT loss keeps them likely.

1106
01:13:05,289 --> 01:13:10,950
The rejected rewards look bigger, but remember, at a beta of 0.5 that's a log ratio of

1107
01:13:10,950 --> 01:13:13,320
about minus 12, smaller than before.

1108
01:13:13,320 --> 01:13:19,178
And the result: the leak is still basically gone, two answers out of 320, and SpellingBee

1109
01:13:19,178 --> 01:13:20,297
is at 78 percent.

1110
01:13:20,297 --> 01:13:26,024
Not quite the 86 we started with, and with a hundred test questions, the noise is about

1111
01:13:26,024 --> 01:13:28,525
four percent, so we did lose a little.

1112
01:13:28,525 --> 01:13:34,252
That's the honest story of preference tuning: it's a tradeoff, you have to measure what

1113
01:13:34,252 --> 01:13:36,885
you might break, and you tune the leash.

1114
01:13:36,885 --> 01:13:38,399
Okay, let's talk to it.

1115
01:13:39,399 --> 01:13:43,863
On our twenty test questions, the leak goes from one in eight to zero.

1116
01:13:43,863 --> 01:13:49,539
On the loop quantum gravity question, with the same seed as before, all eight answers are

1117
01:13:49,539 --> 01:13:50,878
ordinary answers now.

1118
01:13:50,878 --> 01:13:54,513
And strawberry, with quotes, is still eight out of eight.

1119
01:13:54,513 --> 01:13:56,298
So that's preference tuning.

1120
01:13:56,298 --> 01:14:01,910
We never wrote down what a good answer is, we only said which of two answers was better,

1121
01:14:01,910 --> 01:14:07,585
and the model picked it up, while keeping what it knew, as long as we kept it on a leash.

1122
01:14:07,585 --> 01:14:12,835
Exercise: We changed two things at once, the SFT loss and beta.

1123
01:14:12,835 --> 01:14:14,419
Which one mattered?

1124
01:14:14,419 --> 01:14:21,586
Run plain DPO with a beta of 0.5 and no SFT loss, and then the SFT loss with a beta of

1125
01:14:21,586 --> 01:14:24,752
0.1, and measure SpellingBee for both.

1126
01:14:24,752 --> 01:14:32,253
Then do exercises fifteen and seventeen in the notebook: the log probability of an answer,

1127
01:14:32,253 --> 01:14:33,669
and the DPO loss.

1128
01:14:33,669 --> 01:14:36,503
Pause the video and give it a try.

1129
01:14:40,003 --> 01:14:41,722
Okay, so let's zoom out.

1130
01:14:41,722 --> 01:14:47,882
We took a base model, a document simulator, and we gave it a format for conversations,

1131
01:14:47,882 --> 01:14:50,461
with special tokens and a loss mask.

1132
01:14:50,461 --> 01:14:56,263
We built a mixture of data where every part teaches something specific, a packing

1133
01:14:56,263 --> 01:15:01,062
dataloader, and we ran SFT, which is just pretraining on that data.

1134
01:15:01,062 --> 01:15:07,151
We wrote an inference engine with a KV cache and tool use, evaluations, reinforcement

1135
01:15:07,151 --> 01:15:12,237
learning with a group baseline, and preference optimization from pairs.

1136
01:15:12,237 --> 01:15:16,391
And you've now written every one of these pieces yourself.

1137
01:15:16,391 --> 01:15:21,761
Now if you want to post-train your own model, here's the recipe, in the order you'd

1138
01:15:21,761 --> 01:15:22,731
actually do it.

1139
01:15:22,731 --> 01:15:23,895
First, the format.

1140
01:15:23,895 --> 01:15:29,653
Pick your special tokens, and make sure the training data and the prompt at test time are

1141
01:15:29,653 --> 01:15:31,593
rendered by the same function.

1142
01:15:31,593 --> 01:15:37,221
The mask: one for everything the model has to write, including the end of its turn, and

1143
01:15:37,221 --> 01:15:40,391
zero for everything else, including tool outputs.

1144
01:15:40,391 --> 01:15:45,955
Give the new tokens rows in the embedding table, and check the loss at initialization.

1145
01:15:45,955 --> 01:15:51,712
Build a mixture where every dataset has a job, and remember that every dataset shapes the

1146
01:15:51,712 --> 01:15:53,070
model beyond its job.

1147
01:15:53,070 --> 01:15:57,987
Pack conversations into rows, never cut one, and use a gentle learning rate.

1148
01:15:57,987 --> 01:16:03,679
Evaluate before and after every single stage, on held out data, otherwise you don't know

1149
01:16:03,679 --> 01:16:04,520
what you did.

1150
01:16:04,520 --> 01:16:10,278
Use RL where you can check the answer automatically, and where the model already succeeds

1151
01:16:10,278 --> 01:16:10,925
sometimes.

1152
01:16:10,925 --> 01:16:16,682
And use preferences for everything where you can only say which of two answers is better.

1153
01:16:16,682 --> 01:16:22,530
And here's the debugging checklist, which is really just every way things went wrong in

1154
01:16:22,530 --> 01:16:23,403
this lecture.

1155
01:16:23,403 --> 01:16:29,049
If your loss is NaN, look for batches with zero trainable tokens, like our truncated

1156
01:16:29,049 --> 01:16:30,797
multiple choice questions.

1157
01:16:30,797 --> 01:16:35,300
If the first losses are huge, it's probably the new special tokens.

1158
01:16:35,300 --> 01:16:40,812
If the model never stops talking, check that assistant end gets a one in the mask.

1159
01:16:40,812 --> 01:16:46,458
If it has strange habits on unrelated questions, look at your data mixture, like our

1160
01:16:46,458 --> 01:16:47,600
SpellingBee leak.

1161
01:16:47,600 --> 01:16:53,649
If the RL reward doesn't move, the questions are too hard or too easy, and every advantage

1162
01:16:53,649 --> 01:16:54,187
is zero.

1163
01:16:54,187 --> 01:16:59,968
And if the training reward goes up but your held out eval goes down, the model found a

1164
01:16:59,968 --> 01:17:01,581
loophole in your reward.

1165
